  ],
  "decisions": [
    {
      "@id": "decision/0001",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-16T17:00:00Z",
      "run": "run/0000",
      "name": "Semantic search is post-v1",
      "rationale": "The spec itself ships keyword search first and describes semantic search as additive. It needs a bundled llama.cpp runtime, sqlite-vec, a PDO connector override and a scheduler authority -- a large item on top of a large item, with nobody asking for it yet. The schema already carries the columns it would need, so deferring costs no rework.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:8a-semantic-search"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/embedding-provider",
        "item/embedding-sweep",
        "item/hybrid-ranking",
        "item/vector-index-seam"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0002",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-16T17:00:00Z",
      "run": "run/0000",
      "name": "Vision-model OCR is post-v1",
      "rationale": "Optional by design, and it drags in one to two gigabytes of model weights and a hardware-aware picker. Tesseract is the stated default and it works. This lands after the llama.cpp runtime exists for embeddings, so the runtime is paid for once.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:7a-ocr-providers"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/hardware-profile-picker",
        "item/llama-runtime-bundle",
        "item/ocr-provider-seam",
        "item/remote-ocr-providers"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0003",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-16T17:00:00Z",
      "run": "run/0000",
      "name": "PostgreSQL ships with unranked search in v1",
      "rationale": "FTS5 gives SQLite ranked BM25 results; other drivers fall back to a correct-but-unranked LIKE. A ranked tsvector index is a second search implementation to maintain and test, and SQLite is the default everyone runs. v1 documents the limitation instead of hiding it.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:8-search"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/tsvector-index"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0004",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-16T17:00:00Z",
      "run": "run/0000",
      "name": "Legal hold is file-level only in v1",
      "rationale": "Only files carry the column, and a single held file already blocks its whole period from purging -- which is the guard that matters. A subtree-wide hold is a second inheritance model to reason about for no additional protection.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:9-archive-and-purge"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/directory-legal-hold"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0005",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-16T18:25:00Z",
      "run": "run/0002",
      "name": "PostgreSQL's value_string index is its own item, not part of the MySQL fix",
      "rationale": "Fixing the MySQL key-length failure exposed the same defect class on PostgreSQL: it still indexes value_string whole, and its btree tuple ceiling of roughly 2704 bytes is crossed by about nine hundred characters of three-byte content. It was not folded into that fix because the MySQL one had a failing CI leg proving both the bug and the cure, and this has neither -- it fails on insert, only for non-ASCII content, so nothing currently exercises it. Every candidate remedy also costs something real: an expression index on a truncation serves only queries written against that same expression, a hash index gives up ordering and prefix matching, and shortening the column was already rejected for cutting product capacity to dodge a storage-engine limit. Choosing between those without a test to judge them by would be guessing.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:4-data-model"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/pgsql-value-string-index"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0006",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-17T08:05:00Z",
      "run": "run/0005",
      "name": "Sibling names are compared case-insensitively and accent-sensitively, on a persisted normalised key",
      "rationale": "Three actions asked \"does a sibling already hold this name\" with a plain equality comparison, which meant three different things on three drivers: MySQL's utf8mb4_unicode_ci folds case and accents, while SQLite and PostgreSQL compare bytes. Uploading Report.pdf beside report.pdf therefore added a version to the existing document on MySQL and created a second, separate document on the other two -- silently, in both directions. Case-insensitive won because StoreFileVersion is built on \"same name in the same directory is a new version\", so the strict drivers silently fork a document when a tool re-saves it with different casing; because the filesystems people upload from are case-insensitive, making a directory holding both names undownloadable as a folder; and because it ratifies what the driver most likely to be deployed already does. Accent-insensitivity was rejected: resume.pdf and résumé.pdf are distinct names to every filesystem, and MySQL is the outlier in folding them. NFC normalisation is part of the rule rather than an exception to it, because macOS clients submit NFD filenames and byte-comparing the two encodings would split one abstract name in two. The comparison is done on a persisted name_key column rather than in SQL or in PHP: LOWER(name) inherits the column collation on MySQL so it stays accent-insensitive, and SQLite's lower() is ASCII-only without ICU so it would turn SQLite case-sensitive for any non-ASCII letter -- the expression that looks portable is wrong on two of three drivers. Comparing in PHP is correct but cannot use an index to narrow candidates, because on MySQL the prefilter is itself the bug. A persisted key is the only option that also leaves a path to a real database constraint.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:4-data-model"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/name-key",
        "item/content-actions"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0007",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-17T08:05:00Z",
      "run": "run/0005",
      "name": "content-actions is split three ways, because two of its clauses could not be satisfied as written",
      "rationale": "The item bundled rename, move and trash behind a doneWhen with two clauses that no implementation could honour. \"Name collisions throw the existing Duplicate exceptions\" named an outcome that differed by driver and had no agreed meaning until decision/0006; it is now item/name-key and blocks the rest. \"A trashed directory's files vanish from the browser and from search at query level\" described behaviour the codebase does not have and would not gain by soft-deleting one row: DirectoryAccess::resolveViewable collects granted directories' paths and returns every directory prefix-matching one, so a live grandchild of a trashed directory still matches a grant held on a live ancestor, and its files were never removed from search_documents. A file directly inside the trashed directory is hidden only incidentally, by the SoftDeletes global scope on the query that builds the reachable set, which nothing asserts and nobody designed. The answer is also inconsistent: a grant sitting on the trashed directory itself drops the entire subtree, live descendants included, so the same tree resolves differently depending on where the grant hangs. That is a defect, not missing work, so it became item/trash-directory with a test that must fail on main before it passes. The fix belongs in DirectoryAccess and in a cascade at trash time, not in Services\\Search: reach already has exactly one home, and a second reach rule inside Search would fix search alone while leaving the browser and the policy path open. What remains of content-actions is rename and move, which is an S -- MoveDirectory, TrashFile and RestoreFile already exist.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:4-data-model"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/content-actions",
        "item/name-key",
        "item/trash-directory",
        "item/trash-purge-manual",
        "item/trash-view",
        "item/files-three-pane",
        "item/api-content"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0008",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-17T08:40:00Z",
      "run": "run/0006",
      "name": "A replacement index is created before the one it supersedes is dropped",
      "rationale": "The name_key migration dropped the composite (parent_id, name) and (directory_id, name) indexes before creating their name_key equivalents, and MySQL refused with errno 1553: the index was needed by a foreign key constraint. InnoDB requires an index whose leading column is the constrained one, and the old composite index had been quietly serving that role since the tables were created -- nothing named it as the foreign key's index, so nothing suggested it could not simply be replaced. PostgreSQL and SQLite do not require an index for a foreign key at all, so all four of their legs passed and reported nothing; only the two MySQL legs failed, and they took 415 of 452 tests with them because every test runs migrations. Creating the replacement first fixes it, because it leads with the same column and so can carry the constraint before the old index goes. Recorded as a rule rather than a one-off fix: this is the second time a constraint MySQL enforces and the other two drivers ignore has reached a green pull request, after the properties key-length failure, and any future index swap on a constrained column has the same trap.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:4-data-model"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/name-key",
        "item/pgsql-value-string-index"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0009",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-17T10:45:00Z",
      "run": "run/0007",
      "name": "A pre-existing action missing a guard is fixed inside the item that names the guard",
      "rationale": "MoveDirectory had no name collision check at all: moving a directory into a parent that already held that name silently succeeded, leaving two live siblings sharing a name, which spec section 4 says cannot exist. CreateDirectory checked and the move path never did. The rescoped content-actions issue said MoveDirectory already existed and needed only refusal-test coverage, which was wrong, and the subagent implementing the item flagged the contradiction between its brief and the issue rather than silently resolving it either way. The fix belongs in this item and not a new one, because the item's own doneWhen says a rename or move into a taken name throws the existing Duplicate exceptions, and MoveDirectory is one of the two move paths: filing it separately would have satisfied that sentence for MoveFile while the directory equivalent stayed broken. The general rule is that when an item names a guarantee, a pre-existing action that silently lacks it is inside that item's scope, and the issue text is corrected rather than the scope narrowed to match what was already written.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:4-data-model"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/content-actions"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0010",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-17T11:20:00Z",
      "run": "run/0008",
      "name": "doccum states every semantic it relies on; none is inherited from a driver or framework default",
      "rationale": "Seven defects now share one root cause, and decision/0008's index-ordering rule is a special case of it: the code inherited a semantic from a driver or framework default instead of stating it. MySQL's utf8mb4_unicode_ci decided what the same name meant; InnoDB's 3072-byte key limit and PostgreSQL's 2704-byte btree tuple limit decided which values could be stored; InnoDB's rule that a foreign key needs an index was satisfied by an index nobody knew was carrying it; PostgreSQL's LIKE decided that search was case-sensitive; Eloquent's SoftDeletes global scope was accidentally the reach filter for a trashed directory's own files; and deleted_at's second precision was relied on to identify a trash cascade, so a file trashed in the same second as its directory was indistinguishable from one trashed by it. That last one was found by CI inside the very item that fixed the SoftDeletes instance, which is the argument for stating the rule rather than fixing instances. Three rules follow: any comparison of user-supplied text states its folding rule in code and proves it on all six legs; every index is sized against utf8mb4 worst case and expressed driver-conditionally in one place when it exceeds a limit; every foreign key column keeps an index leading with that column that no later swap drops. A fourth, from this run: identity and grouping are carried by a column that means it, never by a timestamp, a collation's opinion, or the absence of a row. item/schema-query-audit makes the next instance fail at CI rather than in an install.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:4-data-model"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/schema-query-audit",
        "item/name-key",
        "item/trash-directory",
        "item/email-folding",
        "item/pgsql-value-string-index"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0011",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-17T12:20:00Z",
      "run": "run/0010",
      "name": "The properties value_string index is equality-only on PostgreSQL, and search-filters must spell it",
      "rationale": "decision/0005 deferred this choice because every remedy cost something and none could be judged without a test. With the test in hand the answer was not a close call: reproducing the failure against a real PostgreSQL returned the engine's own HINT suggesting a function index on an md5 hash, so the chosen fix is the database's recommendation rather than a pick among three guesses. A truncation expression was rejected because PostgreSQL only uses an expression index when the query is spelled with that identical expression, and it stays an approximation needing the real predicate for correctness whenever two values share the truncated prefix -- a cost with no offsetting benefit, since spec section 4 defines this index for equality lookup alone: ordering and ranges live in value_number and value_date, prefix and substring search in search_documents. The index therefore serves equality only, written as md5(value_string) = md5(?) alongside the real value_string = ? predicate, and cannot serve ordering or LIKE at all. item/search-filters must add that second predicate for the string, text and select data types on PostgreSQL or the planner will never pick this index; that obligation is written into the migration where whoever implements it will read it, and was confirmed by EXPLAIN rather than assumed. The regression test was nearly worthless before review: a value built by repeating one character is pglz-compressed by TOAST before storage, so it never reaches the byte width the test means to exercise and passes against the unfixed index. It uses a thousand distinct codepoints instead, and says why.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:4-data-model"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/pgsql-value-string-index",
        "item/search-filters",
        "item/schema-query-audit"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0012",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-17T13:25:00Z",
      "run": "run/0011",
      "name": "A container-smoke assertion is worth only what a mutation says it is worth",
      "rationale": "The rule added with the topbar smoke said to assert the hidden state before an interaction as well as the visible state after, on the reasoning that asserting only the visible state would pass on a container shipping no JavaScript. That reasoning was never tested, and it was wrong. Removing @fluxScripts from the layout and running the smoke against the resulting image failed inside the topbar check on the click, so the visible-after-click assertion is the load-bearing one; the hidden-before assertion passed in that same image with no JavaScript in it at all, because nothing was there to reveal the menu in the first place. A rule derived by argument had been written into CLAUDE.md where fourteen remaining UI items would have copied it. The rule is now the experiment rather than the argument: break the feature deliberately, run the smoke against that image, record the run, and prefer an assertion that requires the feature to do something over one that observes a resting state. The same experiment retired a second unfounded claim, that comparing the version pill against the container catches a stale config cache: tinker and the Blade read the same config in the same process, and the image runs no config:cache, so the check proves only that the pill renders the configured value rather than a literal. It becomes a real cross-source comparison once item/version-from-tag supplies the image label to compare against.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/container-smoke-ci",
        "item/topbar-shell",
        "item/files-three-pane",
        "item/directory-access-ui",
        "item/trash-view",
        "item/home-dashboard",
        "item/search-filters",
        "item/admin-users"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0013",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-17T13:25:00Z",
      "run": "run/0011",
      "name": "Email is folded to lowercase on write by the application, and existing rows are not backfilled",
      "rationale": "Email comparison was left to whatever each driver did: MySQL folded case in the utf8mb4_unicode_ci collation, SQLite and PostgreSQL compared bytes. The defect that hid behind that divergence was worse than a comparison inconsistency. Fortify lowercases a submitted credential before authenticating, and its own registration controller folds before creating, so /register was accidentally safe; but the installer and the self-service email change bypass that controller and stored the address exactly as typed, so an administrator who installed as a mixed-case address could not log in at all on SQLite or PostgreSQL. The account that bootstraps the instance was the one that could be locked out of it. Folding is therefore doccum's own stated rule now, applied by a saving hook on the model and by the same rule at login through Fortify::authenticateUsing, rather than inherited from a config flag this application happens also to set or from a column collation only one driver has. The rule is case only, trim then mb_strtolower: deliberately not NameKey's, which normalises to NFC and is accent-sensitive, because nothing motivates accent folding for an address and inventing a semantic no report backs is what decision/0010 forbids. mb_strtolower rather than SQL LOWER, which is ASCII-only on SQLite without ICU. Rows written before this change keep whatever case they have until next saved, and no backfill migration was written: a bulk lowercase collides with the unique constraint on exactly the installations that have the duplicate-account bug this fixes, and nothing can mechanically choose which of two real accounts wins when roles, directory ownership and audit history all hang off the id. That reconciliation is its own work, filed as issue 76, following the precedent decision/0006 set for name_key of leaving a visible gap rather than a silent one.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:5-access-control"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/email-folding"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0014",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-17T16:25:00Z",
      "run": "run/0012",
      "name": "A directory row must never be force-deleted while files.directory_id cascades",
      "rationale": "files.directory_id is declared with cascadeOnDelete, so force-deleting a directory row removes every descendant file row in SQL, in the database, without any PHP running per file. That path never reaches Services\\DocumentStorage, so every object those files owned is left in the store with nothing referencing it -- and it looks like it worked, because the rows vanish and the interface updates. PeriodPurger is safe only because it walks files one at a time and deletes their objects before their rows; a cascade skips exactly the step that makes it safe. Nothing force-deletes a Directory today, so this is latent rather than live, which is why item/trash-purge-manual deliberately covers files only and says so in its own docblock rather than quietly choosing. It is recorded here because the next work to touch this area is item/trash-view, where purging a directory is the obvious thing to reach for and the obvious implementation is the unsafe one. A safe directory purge walks every descendant file through the file path first; whether the foreign key should stop cascading, so the database refuses the shortcut rather than silently performing it, is open and is the decision/0010 posture applied to a driver convenience. Filed as issue 86.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:9-archive-and-purge"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/trash-purge-manual",
        "item/trash-view"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0015",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-17T17:15:00Z",
      "run": "run/0013",
      "name": "A persistence claim is tested by destroying whatever could be hiding the state, not by restarting it",
      "rationale": "CLAUDE.md said /data was the only persistent volume and listed SQLite first. That was false for the run command CLAUDE.md itself documents. compose.yaml has always set DB_DATABASE; the image never did, so config/database.php fell back to database_path('database.sqlite') and the database lived inside the image layer, while objects and minio.env sat on the volume -- so `docker rm` or an image upgrade brought the instance back half-alive rather than empty, which is worse than empty because it looks like it worked. The entrypoint had been creating /data/doccum.sqlite throughout: a file nothing ever opened. The smoke could not catch this, and the reason is the general lesson. Its persistence step ran `docker restart`, which keeps the container's writable layer, so a database inside the image layer survives it; a step named \"Verify persistence after restart\" passed for its whole life without exercising the property it was named for. Restarting a process tests that the process can restart. It says nothing about where state lives, because everything that would disappear is still there. The step now destroys the container and starts a new one from the same image against the same named volume, which is what persistence has to mean for a container people are told to upgrade by replacing. The two halves check each other and neither would have been enough alone: without the DB_DATABASE default the verify phase cannot log in at all, and without the smoke change nothing would have proven the default mattered. Generalised: for any claim that X survives Y, the test must actually perform Y in the form an operator would, and must be shown to fail when X is not on the durable side of it. Filed as issue 91.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/container-smoke-ci"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0016",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-17T17:15:00Z",
      "run": "run/0013",
      "name": "Green on main is read from a main run, and only the newest one speaks for main",
      "rationale": "The loop reported \"main is green\" every iteration without being entitled to. docs/LOOP.md gated merging on the pull request's checks and then went straight to picking the next item, so nothing ever looked at main afterwards. A pull request's checks run against the pull request's head, not against the main that merging it produces, and the gap is not theoretical: of main's 38 tests.yml push runs up to run 0012, 8 failed and 6 were cancelled, and every one of the five failures that came after CI was established was a merge commit whose pull request had been fully green. The loop now waits for the push run on the merge commit and reads its conclusion, and \"main is green\" may only be said after reading a main run. Applying the rule immediately exposed a second hole in it, which is why this decision says \"the newest one\". Merging pull requests 94 and 95 a few minutes apart meant GitHub's concurrency group cancelled the run on the first merge commit in favour of the second: ba799a9 shows two check runs, both green, because the other nine were cancelled before they started. A poller that asks \"are all the checks on this commit green\" answers yes to that, which is a false green of exactly the kind this decision exists to prevent -- read from a main run, and still wrong. An intermediate merge commit in a back-to-back sequence therefore never gets a verdict and must not be treated as having one; the tip is what has to be green, and a completeness check on the number of checks is part of reading the answer, not an optimisation.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/ci-green-baseline"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0017",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-17T17:15:00Z",
      "run": "run/0013",
      "name": "An item does not close with its own central question unanswered while the answer is already in CI output",
      "rationale": "item/extract-text-flake's doneWhen asked for the intermittent failure to be \"reproduced or the object-store race ruled out ... and which is stated\". It closed saying the cause was unknown. It was not unknown: the diagnostic that item itself added -- printing file_texts.error from the container -- printed the cause on main twice within 25 minutes of the item closing, as \"General error: 5 database is locked\" on an update to the jobs table. Nobody read it. The cause is SQLite write contention, not an object-store race and not MinIO readiness: the single container runs FrankenPHP, two queue workers and the scheduler against one file with queue, cache and session all on the database driver, and config/database.php left busy_timeout and journal_mode null, so SQLite's own default busy timeout of zero applied and the loser of a write race failed instantly instead of waiting. That is decision/0010's pattern for the seventh time, and the backoff added in pull request 83 was treating the symptom, which is why it recovered rather than failing outright. Two rules follow. An item may not be marked Completed while the question its own doneWhen poses is open; \"cause unknown\" closes nothing when a diagnostic added for that cause is still running. And a diagnostic is only worth what someone reads -- adding one and then not looking at its output is the same defect as not adding it, with more confidence attached. Filed as issue 92; the design question of whether queue, cache and session belong on the same SQLite file as the application data is deliberately left open rather than settled here.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:14-testing"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/extract-text-flake",
        "item/container-smoke-ci"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0018",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-17T18:45:00Z",
      "run": "run/0014",
      "name": "A refusal that two mechanisms both produce proves neither of them",
      "rationale": "browser-select-directory-view was a mutations.json entry like any other: an authorize('view', ...) call in selectDirectory(), and a test asserting that selecting an unviewable directory is forbidden. It passed review -- mine -- and the harness rejected it, reporting that the test STILL PASSES with the guard deleted. The reason, read out of the code rather than guessed: selecting a directory renders the detail panel, PropertyPanel::mount() authorises view on the same subject, and it refuses first. The 403 arrives either way and the test cannot tell which produced it. This is decision/0012's sharper corollary. decision/0012 says an assertion must require the feature to DO something rather than observe a resting state; this one says an assertion can require action and still prove nothing, when a second mechanism produces the same observable outcome. No amount of reading the test reveals that -- only mutation does. The resolution matters as much as the finding: the authorize call STAYS, because PropertyPanel refusing is PropertyPanel's concern and this line is what remains true if that ever changes, but its mutations.json entry is deleted, because the entry claimed a proof that did not exist. CLAUDE.md's rule that a guard whose test passes without it is worse than none is about false protection; here the protection was real and the PROOF was the fiction, so the claim was deleted rather than the guard. item/files-actions-ui's doneWhen asked for one entry per authorize call, each shown failing; that is satisfied for seven of eight, with the eighth documented in the code and the test docblock rather than faked.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:14-testing"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/files-actions-ui",
        "item/container-smoke-ci"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0019",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-17T18:45:00Z",
      "run": "run/0014",
      "name": "Build the instrument before the hypothesis, because in this environment nothing else can be checked",
      "rationale": "The container smoke cannot be run locally: there is no vendor/, composer install is blocked by the organisation's egress policy (403 on codeload.github.com, which composer reports as \"Could not authenticate against github.com\"), and the Docker daemon is unavailable. Every smoke change is therefore a speculative push with a three-minute feedback loop, and \"one validated push beats three speculative ones\" is unachievable as stated. This run paid for learning that. An eight-line redirect fix took fifteen CI rounds, and four consecutive hypotheses about the failure were wrong: that waitForResponse('/livewire/upload-file') would settle it (an endpoint assumed about a Livewire version that cannot be inspected here, which on two runs BECAME the failure); that waiting for window.Livewire would (it is set when the script first loads, so after a wire:navigate DOM swap the condition was already true and could not fail); that reproducing main's page sequence would (it did not); and that the two branches showing the symptom were one deterministic defect (one of them did not reproduce). What resolved it was not a better hypothesis but three instruments, each answering in one run what guessing had failed to answer in nine: waitForFileRow(), which distinguished \"nothing was stored\" from an extraction timeout sixty seconds downstream; tinker() raising status, signal and stderr instead of echoing the command back; and uploadAndProveStored(), which reported that attempt 1 stores nothing and attempt 2, 2.5 seconds later, succeeds. The rule this records is ordering, not attitude: when the loop cannot run the thing it is changing, the first push should be the one that makes the next failure legible, not the one that might fix it. The hourly routine now says so.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/container-smoke-ci",
        "item/ci-green-baseline"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0020",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-17T18:50:00Z",
      "run": "run/0014",
      "name": "A trashed subject must not be reindexed, because a queued reindex can outlive the trash",
      "rationale": "SearchProjectionObserver::deleted() forgets a trashed subject's projection inline, and SearchIndexer::forget() removes the index row before the projection row, deliberately, so nothing can match and then vanish. Both are right, and neither is enough. ReindexSearchDocument is dispatched on save and runs on a real queue in the container, so it can be dispatched BEFORE a trash and run AFTER it -- the dispatch from an upload, or one from an ExtractText still in flight -- and handle() called index() unconditionally. index() is an updateOrCreate, so the projection came back and was republished, and the trashed file answered searches again. Trashing is how a person hides a document; an index that keeps serving it defeats the point, which is what that observer's own docblock already warned about in the abstract. The guard goes in the job, where the ordering problem is, and forgets rather than indexes, which also makes the job idempotent whatever order things land in. Guarding SearchIndexer::index() would be the better seam -- it is the single place a projection is published -- but it returns SearchDocument, so the guard means a nullable return and every caller touched, which is not a change to make unverified on a red main in an environment that cannot run the suite; recorded here rather than done quietly. What makes this worth a decision rather than a bug fix is where it hid. QUEUE_CONNECTION is sync in the suite, so every dispatch runs inline and in order, before the trash: the race is unreachable by construction, and 617 tests were green. CLAUDE.md has said since the beginning that the test queue is synchronous and production is not. This is the first run where that sentence cost something, and it is the clearest case yet for the rule that the container is the product -- a green suite is necessary and not sufficient, and the smoke is not a formality but the only place this class of defect can appear.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:8-search"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/files-actions-ui",
        "item/container-smoke-ci",
        "item/trash-directory"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0021",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-17T18:58:00Z",
      "run": "run/0015",
      "name": "\"main is green\" is a claim about main's ref, not about main's commit",
      "rationale": "LOOP.md step 3 told the loop to count check runs on the merge commit and satisfy itself that the count was complete. Check runs attach to a COMMIT, not to a ref, so every branch pointing at that commit contributes its own workflows. After #113 merged, main at a0cf122 read as twelve checks where 58399dc had read as eleven, and the twelfth was pint from format.yml -- which triggers on claude/** and ledger/** and never on main. It was there because this loop's own development branch had been reset onto main and pushed. No wrong conclusion followed: both reads were genuinely green, and eleven was genuinely main's own complete set. The method was wrong, not the answer, which is the only reason it was caught at leisure rather than after a bad merge. The failure it would eventually have produced is the serious one: a threshold like \"at least eleven\" can be satisfied ENTIRELY by another ref's checks while main's own tests run is still in flight, so the loop would report main green on a run nothing had read -- decision/0016's error arriving from the opposite direction, and invisible in exactly the same way. The replacement cannot conflate refs: ask for workflow runs at the SHA, keep head_branch main and event push, and require the tests and ledger runs to conclude success. Those two are the whole of what a main push triggers; format is claude/**|ledger/**, phpstan-baseline is baseline/**, security is pull_request plus a weekly cron, release is tags. A run's own conclusion aggregates its jobs, so no count of checks is needed at all, and a cancelled run reports cancelled rather than looking green -- which also closes the ba799a9 direction the old text had described and left standing.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:14-testing"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/ci-green-baseline",
        "item/container-smoke-ci"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0022",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-17T21:05:00Z",
      "run": "run/0015",
      "name": "Replace resolves its directory from the file, and replace() is its own ability delegating to create()",
      "rationale": "The obvious shape for a Replace control is to authorise create on the directory being browsed and hand StoreFileVersion that same directory. It is broken with no mutation required. After moveFile() the selected file lives in its destination while $this->directory still names the directory being browsed, and the panel goes on showing the moved file -- so the action's (directory_id, name_key) lookup misses, and it takes the create path and writes a NEW file into the browsed directory. The bug the item exists to prevent, reachable by an ordinary user in the unmutated build. So the directory is resolved from the file. Resolving it inline in the component would mean copying liveDirectory() out of the policy, which is the one thing FilePolicy's class docblock exists to prevent, so the resolution lives in a replace() ability that delegates to create() rather than restating files.upload plus Edit -- the same shape download() already uses to delegate to view(), and the property that matters: a future third condition on create(), a quota or a per-directory upload lock, cannot drift away from Replace without someone noticing. replace() carries two refusals, not one, and they are about different things. A cascade-trashed DIRECTORY is refused for the reason view()/update()/move()/legalHold() refuse it. A trashed FILE is refused because StoreFileVersion looks files up through the default non-trashed query, so for a trashed file it would create a fresh file under the same name -- StoreFileVersionTest already proves that is the action's designed behaviour -- and a later RestoreFile would then collide on that name, leaving the trashed original permanently unrestorable. The better fix is structural and is deliberately not taken here: a StoreFileVersion::replace() entry point would make the create path unreachable by type rather than by which string the caller passes. It is filed as issue 115 and blocked on issue 106, because restructuring the seam every upload crosses while a silent-discard bug inside that seam is unexplained would make that bug harder to find, not easier.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:10-user-facing-surface",
        "spec:4-data-model"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/files-versions-replace",
        "item/files-actions-ui"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0023",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-17T21:15:00Z",
      "run": "run/0015",
      "name": "A smoke mutation is read in both directions, because a check can fail for reasons of its own",
      "rationale": "decision/0012 established that a smoke assertion is worth only what a mutation says it is, and described the procedure as pushing a branch with the feature broken and reading the image job. That describes half of a proof. The replace check was written with a checksum triple as its stated evidence and a rendered-row count its own docblock called subordinate. The mutated image failed on the row count and never reached a checksum -- and the correct build failed there too, identically, both timing out on the same selector. A check that fails in both directions proves nothing at all, and this one was minutes from being recorded as evidence with a run URL attached. mutation-check.php has asserted both directions for suite guards since it was written, precisely because a test that fails either way is just broken; the smoke procedure inherited only the failing half, and nothing in the loop compared the two. The cause here was mundane and already known: straight after an upload the main form's file input still displays the chosen filename, so the page carries that text twice and getByText() does not reliably land on the listing's link, so selectFile() never fires and there is no version list to wait for. Issue 107 recorded that same behaviour fooling an upload assertion, and checkTrashRemovesFileFromListingAndSearch() re-navigates before selecting for exactly this reason. Two things follow and both are now in the check. The mutation record names BOTH runs, the passing build and the failing one, because either alone is the same mistake. And the check was reordered so the evidence claimed to be load-bearing is the evidence that fires: it polls the database and reports the checksum triple before touching the DOM, so the mutation names its symptom -- \"replace did not update the document's own current version checksum\" -- instead of timing out on a selector, while the rendered-row assertion survives as its own separate claim about the image. That ordering is run 0014's topbar lesson applied before the fact rather than after it.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:14-testing"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/container-smoke-ci",
        "item/files-versions-replace"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0024",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-17T21:50:00Z",
      "run": "run/0016",
      "name": "A selection supplied by the client must resolve exactly, or nothing happens",
      "rationale": "Browser::bulkTrash() took $selectedIds, resolved them with whereIn('id', ...) and trashed whatever came back. Two things were wrong with that and only the second is obvious in hindsight. It was not scoped to the directory being browsed, although selectFile() scopes its own lookup for precisely this reason -- $selectedIds arrives from the client, and the listing this component renders only ever holds files from one directory, so an id from anywhere else did not come from the UI. Authorisation still gated every file, so nothing was ever exposed, but the reachable surface was wider than the interface implies. The scope had been dropped deliberately, to make a test constructible; that is the wrong direction, and decision/0025 records the half of the problem that motivated it. The second is worse and was found only by reading the first: whereIn() SILENTLY DROPS an id that matches nothing. A crafted id, or a file another user trashed or moved between the listing rendering and the button being clicked, therefore made the method trash a SUBSET of the selection and report success. \"Trash exactly what I selected\" and \"trash whichever of those are still here\" are different promises to make to someone deleting documents, and only the first is safe to make silently. Resolution is now scoped AND exact: the resolved count must equal the selected count or the whole call aborts, with tests for an id from another directory and for one that no longer exists. A concurrent trash by someone else now fails the call rather than half-performing it, which is the right way round -- the selection no longer describes reality, and this method's entire purpose is to be all-or-nothing.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:10-user-facing-surface",
        "spec:5-access-control"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/files-list-sort-select"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0025",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-17T21:51:00Z",
      "run": "run/0016",
      "name": "A doneWhen clause that cannot be constructed is recorded as unprovable, not worked around",
      "rationale": "item/files-list-sort-select asks that bulk trash \"trashes nothing if any ONE is refused\". That case cannot be built. FilePolicy::delete() is files.delete -- a global permission -- plus DirectoryAccess::can(Edit) on the file's directory, and has no per-file component at all, so every file in one directory answers identically for one user: the refusal is all-or-nothing, and one-of-several does not exist. There were three ways out and only one is honest. Weakening the resolution so a file from another directory could be selected makes the case constructible, and that is what was originally done -- it widens the component's reach to satisfy a sentence, which is decision/0024. Writing a mutation entry that claims the stronger property anyway would be a proof of something that cannot happen, which is exactly the fiction run 0014's browser-select-directory-view and run 0015's file-policy-replace-refuses-trashed-directory were both caught being. So the entry claims only what is provable -- remove the authorize pass and a viewer holding view alone trashes every file in the directory -- and that test is isolated, since both files resolve, the exact-resolution guard cannot fire, and TrashFile authorises nothing. The two-pass authorise-all-then-trash-all structure stays regardless of what can be proved about it, because a single loop leaves every file before the refusal already trashed, which satisfies the clause in wording only; it is defensive for when delete() grows a per-file condition, and PurgeFile already refuses a file under legal hold, so trash plausibly should too. LOOP.md step 4 has always said that a clause which cannot be satisfied as written gets said plainly rather than faked. This is the first run that had to do it, and the general rule is worth stating: the item's wording is a claim about the product, and when the product cannot make that claim the ledger records the gap rather than the backlog quietly getting what it asked for.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:14-testing",
        "spec:5-access-control"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/files-list-sort-select",
        "item/files-actions-ui"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0026",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-17T22:30:00Z",
      "run": "run/0016",
      "name": "A retry that reuses wedged state is not a retry, and issue 106 wedges the form",
      "rationale": "Issue 106 was recorded as an upload that is silently discarded -- no request, no error, roughly one attempt in two. The container smoke worked around it by retrying in place, and that workaround was wrong in a way that took a red main to expose. When the upload is swallowed, the component is left believing one is still in flight, so Livewire keeps the submit button DISABLED; every in-place retry then clicks a button that exists, is visible, and will never accept the click, burning Playwright's full thirty-second actionability timeout against a dead control. It happened first on the replace check one merge after a retry was added to it, and then -- once that was fixed by reloading -- immediately on uploadAndProveStored(), the pre-existing helper, on a different form and a different button. Two forms, two buttons, one failure shape: the wedge belongs to the swallowed upload, not to either form. That helper had retried in place since it was written and got away with it only because a second attempt usually landed before the component wedged, which is why uploading three files rather than one was enough to find it. Both now reload between attempts and bound the click, because starting clean does not depend on knowing exactly which state Livewire is stuck in -- and the inference about WHY the button is disabled is the one part of this that was reasoned from the log rather than reproduced, since neither Docker nor Playwright can run in this environment. The larger point is about issue 106 itself, and it is a product finding rather than a testing one: the discard is not merely a lost file, it leaves the operator looking at a dead Upload button with no error and no recourse but a page reload. That is worse than the original symptom and it is the same bug. Three runs of workarounds have now cost an unattributable mutation cycle, a false red on a ledger-only pull request, a red main, and retry scaffolding in two checks, one of which grew a bug of its own. The recommendation recorded on the issue is to diagnose rather than work around, on the grounds that the new symptom is a better lead: a stuck state machine leaves evidence in the browser, where a discarded request left none.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:14-testing",
        "spec:10-user-facing-surface"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/container-smoke-ci",
        "item/files-versions-replace",
        "item/files-list-sort-select"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0027",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-17T23:46:00Z",
      "run": "run/0017",
      "name": "\"No request, no error\" was never observed, and decision/0026 should not have said it",
      "rationale": "container-smoke.mjs carries no page.on() listeners of any kind and never has, so no request and no console message has ever been captured. What was observed is that no files row appears and that the form's submit button is left disabled. The absence of a request is a GUESS about the cause, and it has been steering every workaround: it points diagnosis at \"the request never left the browser\" when the evidence equally fits a request that was made and answered with the state lost. Supersedes the claim in decision/0026, not its finding about the wedge, which was observed on two forms.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:14-testing"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/upload-silent-discard",
        "item/container-smoke-ci"
      ],
      "supersedes": "decision/0026"
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0028",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-17T23:47:00Z",
      "run": "run/0017",
      "name": "The embedded database's transaction mode is stated in this repository, and it is DEFERRED",
      "rationale": "Issue 121 said Laravel's SQLite transaction mode could not be confirmed without vendor/. It is at config/database.php:69, shipped by the Laravel 13 skeleton, and PR 94 rewrote the pragma block three lines above it without touching it. busy_timeout cannot cover a deferred transaction that reads then writes, which is the queue worker reserving a job, so the run 0013 pragma fix was half a fix and the missing half was on the same screen. That makes issue 121 an S -- state IMMEDIATE -- rather than the choice between one worker and moving the queue off SQLite that the issue proposed.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/sqlite-immediate-transactions"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0029",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-17T23:48:00Z",
      "run": "run/0017",
      "name": "Reliability defects in the shipped container are backlog items, not interruptions",
      "rationale": "Issues 74, 121 and 106 become items at orders 23 to 25, ahead of the remaining feature work, so LOOP.md step 4 picks them by its own rule rather than the loop arguing itself into an exception. Three reasons they go first. item/v1-audit is a verification item one place before the tag with no slack to fix anything, so deferring to it schedules a surprise rather than the work. Every remaining UI item extends the smoke and inherits the upload scaffolding and the ambiguity a locked jobs table puts into every mutation run. And all three are defects an operator meets: a Download link that returns an unreachable host, a document whose text is silently lost, a dead Upload button. Precedent exists -- items 13, 16 and 17 were defects given orders ahead of features.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:15-milestones"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/download-reaches-the-browser",
        "item/sqlite-immediate-transactions",
        "item/upload-silent-discard",
        "item/v1-audit"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0030",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T00:35:00Z",
      "run": "run/0017",
      "name": "Issue 106 is a race with the upload itself, and the retries that hid it are removed",
      "rationale": "Measured, not argued. PR 129 shipped instrumentation and nothing else -- listeners on request, response, requestfailed, console and pageerror -- and its second round caught the discard three times in one run. Every discarded upload had its POST to Livewire's upload endpoint answer in about 550ms with the store() commit issued roughly 14ms after it, while the upload was still in flight. Every stored upload had the upload answer in about 18ms with the commit issued after it. Nothing else separates them. A file input posts its bytes the moment it changes and the component property is not populated until that POST answers, so a submit click inside that window runs store() against an empty property: it fails required|file, and the _finishUpload commit landing a moment later re-renders over the validation error. The person sees no file stored and nothing said. replaceFile() posts through the same path and had the same race.\n\nCredit where it belongs: issue 106 section 2 hypothesised a race in these words -- the smoke \"clicks Upload anyway against a form referencing a temporary file that has not landed\" -- eight runs before this. What it got wrong was the address. It treated the race as the smoke's, and prescribed a test-side fix (wait for the upload response instead of networkidle), so the fix landed in the harness and the product kept the defect; the loop then spent three runs and a \"no request, no error\" description walking away from the right answer. An ordinary person choosing a file and clicking Upload inside half a second hits exactly this, and on a slow link the window is seconds wide, not milliseconds. The lesson is not that the race was unforeseen. It is that a race observed through a test gets attributed to the test, and that the question \"could a user do this?\" was never asked of a hypothesis that had already been written down.\n\nNone of the three candidates this loop had been carrying was right on its face. It was not \"never sent\" and not \"sent and failed\": the request was sent and answered 200, and the state was lost between two commits that raced. decision/0027 was right that the standing description was a guess; this is what the measurement returned in its place.\n\nThe first instrumented round still cost a CI round, and for the same reason the bug survived three runs: it filtered requests to a guessed '/livewire/' prefix and printed nothing at all across a run driving the installer, search and the file browser. This install serves those endpoints under a per-deploy hash, /livewire-a49e10a7/. run/0014 records that exact mistake already -- a waitForResponse against a guessed '/livewire/upload-file' became the failure it was written to observe. An instrument may not assume the shape of what it measures, and a negative filter (everything that is not a static asset) assumes nothing.\n\nThe fix is wire:loading.attr=\"disabled\" with wire:target naming the upload PROPERTY on both submit buttons, so the racing click cannot be made. It is asserted against a deliberately stalled upload endpoint, because the real window is about eighteen milliseconds wide; the disabled assertion is load-bearing and the enabled-before and re-enabled-after assertions are labelled in the source as controls, since a resting state proves nothing and a guard that wedged the button shut forever would satisfy the disabled half alone. That check is also the only thing proving Flux forwards those attributes to the real button rather than swallowing them, which is the same question that made data-test unusable on flux:input.\n\nThe consequence for practice is the part worth carrying forward. Both retries built on this bug are deleted: uploadAndProveStored() and checkReplaceAddsASecondVersion() each retried the whole interaction three times, and a retry is what made the race invisible -- the second attempt always ran after the upload had landed, so the check reported success and the defect stayed in the product. Those retries were added in good faith, twice, and each time they moved the failure somewhere else rather than removing it: one wedged on a disabled button, the other turned a ledger-only pull request red. A retry around a nondeterministic interaction converts a product defect into a testing inconvenience and then charges rent. The replace check's max(version_number) assertion tightens from >= 2 to exactly 2 for the same reason, the loose bound having existed only to tolerate a retry landing a third version. The probe itself stays, because the cheapest thing in this whole sequence was the run that measured rather than reasoned.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:10-user-facing-surface",
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/upload-silent-discard",
        "item/container-smoke-ci",
        "item/files-versions-replace"
      ],
      "supersedes": "decision/0026"
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0031",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T00:45:00Z",
      "run": "run/0018",
      "name": "The consult on run/0017: two new items, three folded into existing doneWhen, one ledger field taken",
      "rationale": "Step 8's consult, with every factual claim checked in the tree before it was acted on -- the loop's recorded failure mode is substituting reading for running, and a consultant cannot run anything here either.\n\nTAKEN, and it is the most valuable thing in either run. The verify phase asserted \"object storage persisted\" from page.getByText(FILE_NAME), which is a listing row out of /data/doccum.sqlite; the search assertion on the next line is SQLite too; and checkDownloadReturnsTheUploadedBytes() was called in setup only. Nothing in verify had ever fetched a byte from /data/objects. A replacement container coming back with objects emptied, minio.env lost, or the bucket gone -- DocumentStorage::ensureBucket() is lazy -- passed green while printing the opposite of what it measured, and Dockerfile:85 records the mirror of that failure, which verify DID catch because that direction is visible to a database read. Confirmed at container-smoke.mjs:1846 before acting. This is the topbar lesson in CLAUDE.md again, and it is worse than the topbar case, because there the assertion was merely weak while here the log line named a subsystem the assertion could not reach. Belongs to item/container-smoke-ci; the mutation is on the volume rather than the application, since the claim is about the volume: empty /data/objects between the two containers and require the SQLite assertions to stay green while only the new one reddens, because an undifferentiated red would prove nothing about which check saw what.\n\nTAKEN. Issue 116 becomes item 26, ahead of the remaining UI work. The consult's ordering argument was reachability; the better argument, which neither of us led with, is consistency: replace() refuses a trashed file, update(), move() and legalHold() do not, and delete(), restore() and purge() deliberately must not. Three postures, one of them documented, and a reader cannot tell which applies without reading each method. The case AGAINST was put and is real -- the window needs a second editor trashing the file between two of the first's requests, the actor already holds edit on the directory so nothing is exposed, and restore undoes a rename -- and it loses only because the item is an S and item 26 was going to be the loop's largest remaining piece of UI. The reachability question cannot be settled here, since it is a property of livewire/livewire that needs vendor/, so the item requires the test to assert WHICH guard refused (403 from the policy, not 404 from a scoped restoration query) and to record the answer either way. That is the first concrete use of run/0017's own untaken remedy against over-determination, which is the failure behind all three of this loop's false mutation proofs.\n\nTAKEN. Issue 75 becomes an item. AuthenticateUser's docblock states that accepting a username \"is tracked as its own item rather than folded in here\", and no item mentioned username at all -- checked by grep over the ledger. A spec 10 clause was owned by nobody while the code asserted otherwise, and item/v1-audit sits one place before the tag with no slack to fix anything, which is decision/0029's argument repeating. login.blade.php:18 is type=\"email\", so the input rejects a username before any of this is reached; that is part of the item.\n\nDECLINED, folded into existing doneWhen instead of becoming items. Issue 115 (no StoreFileVersion::replace() entry point) is compensated at Browser.php:239 and gains a second caller only at item/api-presigned-upload, so it goes in that item's doneWhen. Issue 109 (404 vs 403 on move destinations) is not fixable in moveFile() alone: routes/web.php route-binds {directory} and Browser::mount() authorises after, so the URL already distinguishes absent from unreachable and FileBrowserTest asserts exactly that -- it is a policy for the whole surface and belongs in a Decision when the API item starts, not a patch to one method. Issue 86 (force-deleting a directory orphans objects) is latent: nothing in app/ force-deletes a directory, and the first item that could add the caller is item/trash-view, so it goes in that item's doneWhen. Recording these as folded rather than as items is the point -- three issues that each looked like work are one line each in someone else's acceptance criteria.\n\nLEDGER VOCABULARY. An Issue node type is declined: every item already carries url, and a node would mirror GitHub state the validator cannot check, so it would rot silently. Issue 74's miss was triage, not schema, and the remedy is a LOOP.md step-1 line -- list open issues whose URL no item carries, and dispose of each. mutations[] on PullRequest is wanted, as {id, guardsRun} where id must exist in .github/mutations.json, because the validator can check that and it makes \"the guards job is the only admissible evidence for a mutation claim\" mechanical rather than a habit. mainRun on Run is wanted for the same reason, as a run URL rather than a timestamp: run/0016 narrates a red main in prose and nothing structured says whether it ended green, and \"main is green\" is the one claim this loop has already broken once. ciRounds is declined -- a count without a cause is not insight. Capping Decision rationale is declined, and decision/0030 is the argument: the defect in decision/0026 was a SHORT claim that had never been observed, not a long one. name carries the one-line claim; rationale stays free.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:5-access-control",
        "spec:10-user-facing-surface",
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/container-smoke-ci",
        "item/file-policy-trashed-file-guards",
        "item/login-by-username",
        "item/api-presigned-upload",
        "item/trash-view",
        "item/v1-audit"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0032",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T01:08:00Z",
      "run": "run/0018",
      "name": "Livewire does not scope its model-property restoration, and adding a guard can de-fang an older one",
      "rationale": "Two findings from item/file-policy-trashed-file-guards, both measured, and the second has no precedent in this ledger.\n\nFIRST: Livewire's restoration of a public Model property does NOT apply the SoftDeletingScope. It hands the trashed model back, so Browser::renameFile() reaches authorize('update', ...) and the policy is what refuses. The test was written to assert a SPECIFIC status because 403 and 404 mean opposite things here -- 403 that the policy refused, 404 that renameFile()'s own null check answered because the model was never hydrated -- and assertForbidden()-or-404 would have passed in both worlds and settled nothing. CI answered 403 green on the first run across five test jobs. Issue 116 was therefore REACHABLE rather than theoretical: a file trashed by another user between two of this user's requests really would have been renameable. It also retroactively vindicates replace()'s guard, which had rested on this same unread fact since item/files-versions-replace.\n\nWhat makes it worth a Decision rather than a code comment is that the reasoning went the other way, twice and independently. The implementing worker argued for 404 from the implicit-route-model-binding analogy, said so in its own report, and then flagged that it had asserted 403 anyway -- an honest contradiction it could have quietly smoothed over. The design consult argued for 404 from newQueryForRestoration(). Neither could read Livewire's source, because there is no vendor/ in this environment. Both were wrong. That is the third time in this loop a plausible chain of reasoning about framework internals has been overturned by a single CI run (decision/0028 and decision/0030 are the others), and the pattern is now firm enough to act on: a claim about framework behaviour that cannot be read here is not an argument to be won, it is a measurement to be taken, and the cheapest measurement is a test that asserts the discriminating observable.\n\nThe item's own shape is the generalisable part. The test was SPLIT: a guarantee half asserting the security property (a file trashed on its own is not renameable through the browser), which is true under both mechanisms and stays green whichever CI reported; and a discriminant half asserting the one thing that differs. Conflating them is what forces a coin flip, and a coin flip on an assertion is how an unverified claim gets written down as a fact. Separating them means the guarantee never depends on the unknown, and the unknown gets settled without risking the guarantee.\n\nSECOND, and this one is new: ADDING A CORRECT GUARD CAN SILENTLY DE-FANG AN OLDER GUARD'S PROOF. The guards job failed its first round not on any of the three new mutations -- all three passed -- but on three PRE-EXISTING ones: file-policy-{update,move,legal-hold}-refuses-trashed-directory all reported \"STILL PASSES with the guard deleted\". TrashDirectory cascades onto every descendant file, so a cascade-trashed file is itself trashed; the new trashed-FILE guard runs before liveDirectory() and refuses on its own; and deleting the directory guard stopped changing the outcome. Three tests that were genuinely load-bearing the day before stopped being so, with no change to their own code, and the suite stayed green the entire time.\n\nThis is over-determination arriving from a direction the loop had not considered. Every previous instance (run/0014's browser-select-directory-view, run/0015's, and replace()'s own) was a test that never isolated its guard to begin with. This one was correct when written and was invalidated by a later, unrelated-looking, entirely correct change. It follows that a mutation entry is not a permanent certificate: it is a claim about the code as it stood, and any new refusal placed EARLIER in the same method can void it. The practical rule is that adding a guard to a method means re-reading every existing mutation entry for that method, and the guards job is what makes that mechanical rather than a matter of remembering -- it is the only instrument in this project that can see the failure at all.\n\nThe fix is the isolation replace()'s equivalent test already carried: restore the file, leave the directory trashed, and assert $live->trashed() is false before the real assertion so the premise cannot rot silently. It is a reachable state rather than a contrivance, because restore(), delete() and purge() resolve the directory withTrashed() precisely so a cascade-trashed file can be restored one at a time.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:5-access-control"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/file-policy-trashed-file-guards",
        "item/files-versions-replace",
        "item/file-policy-null-directory"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0033",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T01:55:00Z",
      "run": "run/0018",
      "name": "A smoke check that leaves the browser mid-navigation blames the next check",
      "rationale": "item/files-three-pane's breadcrumb check took four CI rounds and only the fourth produced evidence. The three before it are the finding.\n\nROUNDS 1-2. The check failed IDENTICALLY on the correct build and the mutated one, deterministically, three runs, same locator. A mutation that reddens both directions distinguishes nothing, so nothing was recorded -- which is the rule working, since the tempting reading was \"mutated build fails, therefore the assertion is load-bearing\". Two diagnoses were wrong before measurement: a stale DirectoryAccess memoisation (disproved by reading the provider, where Directory::saved($flushAccess) is registered) and a flake (disproved by three identical failures; CLAUDE.md is explicit that flake is a diagnosis to be earned).\n\nThe cause was a wait that did not discriminate. After clicking into a subdirectory the check waited for the 'New folder' field to be visible -- and that field is on EVERY directory page, so the wait was satisfied by the page being left, before wire:navigate swapped the DOM. fill() then wrote into a field about to be destroyed and the subdirectory was never created. This is decision/0012's topbar lesson in different clothes: a signal already true before the action proves nothing about the action. The comment being replaced even claimed it waited on \"the NEXT page's own content\" -- the intent was right and the chosen signal did not discriminate, which is precisely why that lesson keeps needing to be relearned rather than remembered.\n\nROUND 3 IS THE PART WORTH CARRYING FORWARD. With the two navigations fixed the check passed, and then a DIFFERENT check failed: the upload guard, reporting \"issue #106 is reachable again\". That guard was present and correct at browser.blade.php:223-224 and nothing about it had changed. The breadcrumb check's FINAL wait was the same non-discriminating kind, so the function returned mid-navigation; the upload check then began on the outgoing page, its upload fired, and wire:navigate swapped in a fresh Upload button with no upload in flight, which reads enabled.\n\nSo a non-discriminating wait does not merely fail its own check. It hands a FALSE ACCUSATION to whatever runs next, naming a security guard that was never broken. A flake costs a round; this sends the next reader into the wrong file with a plausible story. It was caught only because the blade was read before the message was believed, which is the same discipline that caught decision/0032's over-determination and decision/0030's misattributed race.\n\nThe rule, stated so it need not be rediscovered: EVERY page transition in a smoke check waits on something true only at the destination, and a check may not return while a navigation it started is still in flight. Two corollaries. First, never wait on the thing under test -- the breadcrumb was both the subject and part of the navigation wait, which is why a breadcrumb defect surfaced as an opaque locator timeout instead of at the count assertion written to catch it; removing that dependency is what moved the mutated failure to a named \"got 1\". Second, when a wait of this shape is found, audit every transition in the function rather than the ones observed failing: two were fixed in one round and the third, untouched because it had not yet failed, produced round four.\n\nThe evidence that finally counted: correct build green with \"breadcrumb shows all 3 ancestors two levels into the tree\", mutated build red with \"expected 3 breadcrumb items ... got 1\". The count assertion is load-bearing, measured rather than predicted -- the implementing worker had named it as its best guess and explicitly declined to claim it proven, which was the right posture and is why the claim was checked instead of inherited.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:10-user-facing-surface",
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/files-three-pane",
        "item/container-smoke-ci",
        "item/upload-silent-discard"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0034",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T02:42:00Z",
      "run": "run/0018",
      "name": "A UI item that makes a latent policy gap reachable must close it, and a mutation must break the step the check can still reach past",
      "rationale": "Two findings from item/directory-access-ui, and the first is the one that mattered.\n\nTHE ESCALATION. DirectoryPolicy::manageAccess() required only AccessLevel::Manage, with no Spatie permission. move(), delete() and restore() all require directories.manage, and spec §5 groups them as ONE capability -- \"grant/revoke access, move or delete the directory itself\" -- so this was the single sibling skipping layer 1, against CLAUDE.md's binding two-layer rule. It was the worst one to leave open, because granting access is how every other restriction gets handed to someone else.\n\nIt was reachable by every user rather than in some corner: MEMBER_PERMISSIONS carries no directories.manage, and spec §5 also says every user holds manage on their own home directory through an ordinary grant. So any member could hand anyone any level on their own subtree while being refused the move and delete the same sentence groups with it.\n\nThe rule this establishes is about WHEN to fix rather than what: the gap was latent only because nothing called the ability from the UI, and this item is precisely what would have made it live. Shipping the UI and filing the hole would have meant knowingly shipping the escalation, so a UI item that makes a latent policy gap reachable closes it in the same change. Nothing was taken away by doing so -- with no UI, nobody could grant through it -- but it did settle a product question by implication, that sharing is an administrator's act in v1, and that is filed as issue 144 rather than left as an inference. If self-service sharing is wanted the answer is a permission of its own that members hold, never a layer removed.\n\nThe implementing worker found this and refused to resolve it inside the item, flagging it instead. That was the right call twice over: it was not the worker's decision to make, and the flag is what made the fix land with the UI rather than after it.\n\nTwo fixture traps came with it, both of which would have produced a green test proving nothing. A permission-alone fixture must NOT be an admin, because DirectoryAccess::resolve() short-circuits directories.view-all to AccessLevel::Manage on every directory, so an admin passes the level check by bypass. And the mutation anchor for the new clause had to sit INSIDE the return expression: the clause is textually identical to restore()'s and delete()'s and mutation-check.php matches by substring, so a bare anchor hit three sites, while anchoring above the return would leave a statement with no return and the mutation would \"fail\" as a TypeError -- testing that PHP has types rather than that the test catches a missing layer. Verified by applying the remove string and running php -l on the result.\n\nSECOND: MUTATE THE STEP THE CHECK CAN STILL REACH PAST. A smoke check with two stages -- grant then revoke -- has two candidate mutations and they are not equivalent. Breaking the FIRST leaves nothing for the second to act on, so the check dies on a locator before any assertion runs and the failure names nothing; that is exactly how item/files-three-pane's first two mutation rounds were wasted (decision/0033). Breaking the SECOND lets the first half pass and PRINT, so the failure arrives at a named assertion with the first half's success as context. The mutated run reads \"granting access through the panel wrote exactly one DirectoryGrant row -- OK\" followed by \"revoking access through the panel did not remove the DirectoryGrant row -- raw output: COUNT:1\", and the first line is what makes the second attributable to the one line that changed rather than merely co-occurring with it.\n\nRecorded with a correction that belongs on the record: decision/0033's rule -- never wait on the thing under test -- was broken here, one item after being written, by Promise.all-ing the Revoke click with a wait for the grant row to become detached, which is precisely what revoking does. The first mutation round therefore failed at \"locator.waitFor: Timeout 10000ms exceeded ... 25 x locator resolved to visible <li data-test=\\\"directory-grant-row\\\">\" instead of at the assertion. Knowing a rule is not the same as applying it; it took meeting the identical opaque-timeout shape a second time to look for the pattern rather than recognise it afterwards. Database evidence first and DOM second, as checkBulkTrashLeavesUnselectedFilesAlone()'s docblock already said.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:5-access-control",
        "spec:10-user-facing-surface"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/directory-access-ui",
        "item/files-three-pane",
        "item/container-smoke-ci"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0035",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T03:25:00Z",
      "run": "run/0018",
      "name": "A mutation that dies before its assertion is void, not negative -- and a fix that removes a proof must say so, then re-earn it",
      "rationale": "Three findings from item/trash-view, and the order they arrived in is the finding about the loop itself: the mutation meant to prove the item's own assertion instead exposed a defect in the harness, which exposed an unproven claim underneath it.\n\nA MUTATION THAT DIES BEFORE ITS ASSERTION IS VOID, NOT NEGATIVE. PR 148 neutered PurgeFile::handle() and its image job went red -- which looks, at a glance, exactly like the verdict it was opened to collect. It was not. It died in checkTrashViewRestoreAndPurge()'s SETUP, on the second of its two uploads, before either click. Red is not evidence; red AT THE NAMED ASSERTION is. Reading the conclusion rather than the log would have recorded a proof that did not exist, and it is worth saying how close that was: the run failed, on the mutation branch, in the check the mutation targets. decision/0033 already says a mutation that reddens both directions distinguishes nothing; this is its neighbour -- a mutation that never reaches its assertion distinguishes nothing either, and it is harder to notice, because the failure is in the right file.\n\nAN ATTRIBUTE CANNOT SAY WHICH UPLOAD FINISHED. The cause was clickOnceUploadSettles(), added by PR 129 to fix the race decision/0030 records. It timed the submit click off the submit button's disabled attribute: wait for the edge, wait for it to clear, click. checkTrashViewRestoreAndPurge() is the first check that uploads TWO files into one page, and a poll that latches onto the first enable it sees cannot tell \"this upload has finished\" from \"some upload has finished\" -- neither the tail of the previous upload nor a gap between _startUpload's response and the upload POST is distinguishable from the real edge. The probe caught it exactly: the submit commit went out 67ms BEFORE the bytes were acknowledged, so store() ran against an empty property, which is issue 106's discard reached through the harness rather than the product. setFileAndWaitForUpload() now waits on the upload's own request lifecycle, armed before setInputFiles() because a file input posts on change. The general form: wait on the lifecycle of the thing you are waiting for, never on a rendering of its state, because a state can be produced by more than one cause and a request cannot.\n\nA FIX THAT REMOVES A PROOF MUST SAY SO, AND THEN RE-EARN IT. That repair had a cost that was nearly missed, and was caught only while correcting the helper's docblock rather than while writing the fix: with the bytes acknowledged before the click, uploadAndProveStored() is no longer sensitive to the product guard at all -- an undisabled button stores fine there. Its docblock had claimed the opposite in as many words (\"if the guard is removed ... some upload in this run stores nothing and the run says so\"), which was true before the fix and false after it. That left issue 106's entire guard resting on ONE assertion, in checkUploadButtonIsDisabledWhileTheFileIsStillUploading() -- and decision/0030 had called that assertion load-bearing on reasoning, with no run behind it. Tolerable while a second check was also sensitive; not tolerable once it was the only one. PR 149 removed wire:loading.attr=\"disabled\" wire:target=\"upload\" from the Upload button and nothing else, and the check failed with its own message, after printing its control line. The claim is now on a run. It also answered the half the check's own error text cannot distinguish: Flux does forward wire:loading.attr to the real button, since removing the attribute is what turned it red.\n\nWHAT IS STILL NOT PROVEN, RECORDED RATHER THAN ROUNDED UP. RESTORE_LIVE:yes remains a prediction. It passed in both directions of PR 148 -- necessarily, since neutering purge is what let the restore half print at all and made the failure attributable. CLAUDE.md requires at least one shown-to-fail assertion per v1 UI item and PURGED_GONE:yes is that one; it is not stretched to cover its neighbour.\n\nTHE COST. Two mutation cycles for one item, one of them spent entirely on discovering that the first was void. That is the correct price. The alternative on offer was a green PR carrying a smoke check whose evidence nobody had, resting on a guard whose proof nobody had, with a docblock asserting both.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:10-user-facing-surface"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/trash-view",
        "item/upload-silent-discard",
        "item/container-smoke-ci"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0036",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T03:40:00Z",
      "run": "run/0018",
      "name": "Sharing a directory is an administrator act in v1, and Manage on your own home therefore gates nothing beyond Edit",
      "rationale": "Issue 144 asks whether an ordinary member should be able to share their own home directory. It is a real question and the spec answers it two ways, so the answer has to be written down rather than left to whichever code was touched last.\n\nTHE TENSION, STATED. Spec section 5 defines the manage LEVEL as \"grant/revoke access, move or delete the directory itself\", and gives every user manage on their own home directory. The same section lists the permission set, and there is no directories.share in it. CLAUDE.md's two-layer rule is binding: a Spatie permission AND directory_access, both. RolesAndPermissionsSeeder gives the member role upload, delete, restore and directories.create -- not directories.manage. So for a non-admin member, the manage level on their own home now conveys nothing beyond edit. That is not a bug introduced by decision/0034's fix; it is what the spec's own closed permission list produces once the layer that was missing is restored.\n\nTHE DECISION: sharing is an administrator act in v1, deliberately, and the current behaviour is correct rather than incidental.\n\nThree reasons, in the order they carry weight.\n\nIt is what the product is. CLAUDE.md's first line says \"governed properties, permission-filtered search\". Governed. A system where any member can hand any level of their own subtree to anyone is a different trust model wearing the same UI, not a relaxed version of this one.\n\nIt is additive later and breaking now. Adding directories.share to the member role after v1 is a seeder line and a policy method. Removing it after people have shared things revokes a permission and strands the grants made under it. When one direction is reversible and the other is not, ship the reversible one.\n\nThe alternative would widen the spec without an audit. item/v1-audit walks the spec's permission list. Inventing a permission the spec does not name, somewhere in the thirties, is precisely the un-audited widening that item exists to catch -- and decision/0034 closed a hole created by exactly one such inconsistency between a level and a permission.\n\nWHAT THIS IS NOT. It is not a claim that the manage level is meaningless: an administrator holds directories.manage and reaches every directory through the directories.view-all bypass, so grant and revoke work in the product today, which is what item/directory-access-ui shipped and mutation-proved. It is only a member's manage on their own home that is inert.\n\nTHE SHAPE OF THE FIX, FOR WHEN IT COMES. item/directory-share-permission, post-v1: a permission of its own gating manageAccess() alone, never a layer removed from it, and never directories.manage granted to members wholesale -- that would also hand them move and delete on every directory they can reach. The distinction between \"may share\" and \"may restructure\" is the whole reason it needs its own permission rather than a reused one.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:5-access-control"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/directory-access-ui",
        "item/directory-share-permission"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0037",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T03:55:00Z",
      "run": "run/0018",
      "name": "A guard whose null branch is dead in production proves nothing, and a remedy named before the evidence is the one reached for under pressure",
      "rationale": "item/download-missing-object was scoped as one change and was two, and CI proved that neither half fixes anything alone. The measurements, in the order they arrived:\n\nTHE HALF THAT WAS SPECIFIED. Open the object's stream BEFORE constructing the response, so a missing object can choose a status. Shipped on its own in the first round: every one of the six test jobs answered 500 where the tests expect 502.\n\nTHE HALF NOBODY HAD NOTICED. config/filesystems.php sets 'throw' => true on the documents disk, so Laravel's FilesystemAdapter::readStream() RETHROWS Flysystem's UnableToReadFile instead of returning null. The `if ($stream === null)` guard in DocumentStorage::readStream() has therefore been dead code in the shipped product for as long as that flag has been set. The typed ObjectMissingFromStorage the first round introduced could not be thrown by any real install, so converting only the null branch would have turned the suite green against a code path nobody takes -- a fix reporting success and changing nothing, which is exactly what CLAUDE.md means by a green suite being necessary and not sufficient. readStream() now converts both signals, because which one a caller gets depends on disk config rather than on anything the caller controls, and a provider seam owes its callers one answer.\n\nTHE MUTATION MEASURED SOMETHING THE ISSUE HAD ONLY GUESSED. PR 154 put readStream() back inside the streamDownload() callback, leaving the seam conversion in place and leaving FileVersionDownloadController untouched as a control. The control passed; the mutated route's test failed with HTTP 200, not 500. So with the stream opened inside the callback, the status line commits as SUCCESS before the missing object is ever discovered: on that server the failure is invisible to any client checking only the status code. Issue 134's reported 500 really was an artefact of FrankenPHP's header-flush timing, which the item asserted and could not show. A 500 would have been a weaker result than the 200 that actually came back.\n\nSo: open-early without convert-at-seam answers 500; convert-at-seam without open-early answers 200; both answer 502. Either half alone would have LOOKED like progress on the issue as written.\n\nTHE REMEDY I PRE-COMMITTED TO WAS THE WRONG ONE. The pull request body flagged the Storage::fake()/'throw' interaction as unverified -- correctly, since vendor/ is absent here and neither the worker nor I could read or execute the source -- and then said that if it went the other way the fix would be a one-line Storage::fake('documents', ['throw' => false]). That would have made the tests pass by configuring the fake to behave UNLIKE the real disk, hiding the production defect completely. Flagging the uncertainty was right and is worth continuing to do; naming a remedy in advance of the evidence was not, because the named remedy is what gets reached for under time pressure when the failure arrives. Flag the uncertainty, and say what would DISTINGUISH the cases, not what to do about one of them.\n\nWHAT IS NOT PROVEN. The Log::shouldHaveReceived('error') expectation rode along with the status assertion in the same failure and is not independently shown to fail. The status assertion is the one this item can claim.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:6-storage-and-period-partitioning"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/download-missing-object",
        "item/download-reaches-the-browser"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0038",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T04:40:00Z",
      "run": "run/0018",
      "name": "A mutation run is a ledger node with a verdict, and negative is the good outcome",
      "rationale": "item/ledger-mutation-nodes exists because this run nearly recorded a proof that did not exist, and because a decision node four items earlier already had.\n\nWHAT THE LEDGER COULD NOT SAY. A hand-run image mutation was a PullRequest node with state closed and a name beginning MUTATION -- DO NOT MERGE. From the vocabulary alone that is indistinguishable from an abandoned branch, and nothing recorded the VERDICT at all. Three things this run produced could not be expressed: PR 148's first head went red, on the mutation branch, in the check the mutation targets, and proved nothing because it died in the check's setup; decision/0030 called an assertion load-bearing on reasoning rather than on a run, and that went unnoticed for four items; and RESTORE_LIVE:yes is a prediction correctly labelled in prose, in a pull request comment and in a decision node -- prose being exactly where the previous one hid.\n\nTHE SHAPE, AND WHY MUTATION IS NOT A FIELD ON PULLREQUEST. A void run and its rebuild share one PR number, so a mutation is not a pull request. mutation/0007 (void, head e80ee98, check null) and mutation/0008 (negative, head 22d0e37, supersedes mutation/0007) are the pair the vocabulary exists for, and the second is what lets the first be recorded honestly rather than quietly force-pushed away.\n\nTHE WORD NEGATIVE IS DELIBERATE AND STAYS UNCOMFORTABLE. negative is the GOOD outcome: the check went red when the guard was removed. positive means the guard is not load-bearing and blocks its item. void means no evidence either way. Naming the good outcome \"passed\" would let a reader skim the field and get it backwards, which is the failure this whole item is about.\n\nTHE RULES BITE, AND THAT WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSERTED. With the grandfather constant set to -1, the validator rejects the real ledger with 39 issues -- four Completed spec:10 items with no negative Mutation, and thirty-five merged PullRequests lacking mainConclusion. Un-superseding mutation/0007 rejects item/trash-view's Completion by name. Nulling a negative verdict's check is rejected, because a failure that names no assertion is a locator timeout wearing a verdict. Each was broken against the real ledger and restored, twice: once by the implementing worker and once independently here, because the rule that a prediction is not a finding applies to the item that introduces it more than to any other.\n\nTHE GRANDFATHER IS HONEST, NOT CONVENIENT. Four Completed spec:10 items have no mutation node, and TWO of them completed in run/0017, the same run as item/upload-silent-discard, which has one. Simultaneous items cannot be separated by a date or a run boundary, so inventing a cutoff would have been a fiction that happened to produce the right answer. The rules instead count from the run after the one that introduced them, and the -1 run above is the evidence that this grandfathers a known four rather than silencing everything.\n\nA MISCLASSIFICATION OF MINE, CAUGHT BY THE RULE IT WOULD HAVE BROKEN. I filed this item as spec:10-user-facing-surface. It is a validator change; item/container-smoke-ci, its closest sibling, is spec:13-infrastructure. Rule 1 keys off spec:10, so the wrong tag would have demanded a container-smoke mutation for a change that cannot sensibly carry one -- shielded only by the grandfather, which is accidental protection resting on a mistake. Fixed before completion, while the item was still a backlog entry rather than history.\n\nWHAT THIS ITEM DELIBERATELY DID NOT DO. mainRunUrl and mainConclusion are vocabulary populated on nothing: the nine mutation PRs are closed-unmerged and have no main run to read, and the thirty-five historical merged PRs were left alone because history is append-only. Decision.evidence is retrofitted onto nothing, decision/0030 included -- the absence is the signal the item argued for, so leaving it absent is the design rather than an omission. And the 39 mechanical entries in mutations.json get no nodes, because mutation-check.php re-proves them every CI run; by the same argument MutationNodeRulesTest re-proves these three rules every run, so this item needs no mutation node of its own either.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/ledger-mutation-nodes",
        "item/trash-view",
        "item/upload-silent-discard"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0039",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T04:50:00Z",
      "run": "run/0018",
      "name": "The DOM is not evidence that a write landed, and a poll for the row is a wait rather than the retry #129 removed",
      "rationale": "main went red at 9e2f7af, a merge whose entire diff was two .jsonld files, and the failure was an upload in checkReplaceAddsASecondVersion() reporting no files row. That is the third time this run a ledger-only merge has reddened main through the smoke's upload path, and this time the cause was not the one the previous two taught.\n\nNOT A DROPPED CLICK, AND THE TIMESTAMPS SETTLE IT. The gate in uploadAndProveStored() was getByText(name) with a 10 000ms timeout, followed by ONE read of the database. The probe shows the submit commit dispatched at 20.7111 and the check giving up at 21.2657 -- 555ms later. A getByText that had genuinely timed out would have failed at 30.7. Failing at 21.26 proves it matched almost immediately, because the chosen filename is on the page as soon as the form re-renders, and the single read then arrived before store() had finished writing. The DOM was never evidence of storage -- which is precisely why fileRowExists() exists -- and the defect was letting the DOM decide WHEN to ask.\n\nA RACE THAT HAD BEEN WINNING ON LUCK. This gate is old and has passed hundreds of times. Nothing in the preceding hour caused it; the upload-lifecycle wait added earlier lengthened the surrounding timing enough to lose a race that was always losable. Worth stating because the tempting reading -- \"the last change broke it\" -- would have sent the fix to the wrong place, which is the shape of decision/0030's mistake.\n\nWAIT VERSUS RETRY, AGAIN, AND THE DISTINCTION IS LOAD-BEARING. The fix polls the database to a deadline. It does not upload again and does not click again. That asymmetry is what keeps it honest: a retry re-attempts the upload and can convert a timing defect into a green run, which is how issue 106 survived three rounds and why PR 129 removed the retry. A poll cannot, because a click that was dropped writes no row however long anyone waits -- so the failure mode the no-retry rule exists to catch is still caught, and only the failure mode that was never real (the read outrunning the write) is removed.\n\nA COST OF MY OWN FIX, PAID BACK. clickOnceUploadSettles() waited up to 5 000ms to observe the button's disabled edge, tolerating its absence. Once setFileAndWaitForUpload() began waiting the upload-file POST out upstream, that edge was already gone by the time the loop ran, so \"not seen\" became the NORMAL case and the full budget was burned before every click in the run -- the 5.7 second gap visible in the trace above. Reduced to 750ms. The wait that matters moved upstream; what remains catches a button wedged shut by a stuck loading state, which shows up at once or not at all.\n\nWHAT A GREEN RUN AFTERWARDS DOES AND DOES NOT PROVE. main came back green on 221b30e across all ten jobs. That is not evidence the race is closed, because the old gate passed hundreds of times before it lost. What justifies the fix is structural: a passing run now means the row exists, where before it meant the filename had rendered. The claim recorded here is that the specific defect is closed by construction, not that the smoke is reliable.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/container-smoke-ci",
        "item/upload-silent-discard"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0040",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T06:15:00Z",
      "run": "run/0019",
      "name": "A mutation picked so only the assertion under test can catch it proves something about the mutation, not the assertion",
      "rationale": "The first Mutation node required by rule 1 rather than volunteered, and the first whose result was deliberately not engineered to flatter the assertion it tests.\n\nWHAT WAS BROKEN. Home\\Index was left untouched -- the query, the DirectoryAccess whereIn and the SoftDeletes scope all still ran, and $recentFiles was still populated. Only the view stopped rendering rows, so /dashboard still answered 200 with its heading, its quick search and its empty state. That is the regression shape the container smoke exists for: a page that renders perfectly well without the thing it is for.\n\nTHE VERDICT. image went red inside checkHomeDashboardShowsRecentUpload(), immediately after that check's own console line, with every preceding check green -- so the harness reached its subject rather than dying on the way, which is the void-versus-negative distinction decision/0035 was written for.\n\nWHAT IT DOES NOT PROVE, WHICH IS THE POINT. The test matrix went red too. HomeDashboardTest asserts rendered output (assertSee at lines 85 and 132), so it is independently sensitive to exactly this breakage. The smoke assertion is real but NOT the only guard covering it. A mutation chosen so that nothing except the assertion under test can catch it proves something about the mutation rather than about the assertion; this one was picked to answer the question, and part of the answer was unflattering. Recorded rather than omitted, because the omission would have been invisible and the claim would have read stronger than the evidence.\n\nA FLAW IT EXPOSED IN THE HARNESS, CAUGHT BY A RULE SHIPPED TWO ITEMS EARLIER. The failure arrived as a bare \"locator.waitFor: Timeout 10000ms exceeded\", naming no assertion at all. It was attributable only because that check's console line happened to be the last thing printed -- luck, not evidence -- and rule 3 of the vocabulary introduced by item/ledger-mutation-nodes says in as many words that a failure naming no assertion is not evidence. The check now fails with \"Home listed no recent-files row for <name>\" plus a container dump, like every other check in the file. The assertion is unchanged, same locator and same wait, so the verdict still describes what merged; it was not re-run over an error string, and that is stated rather than implied.\n\nTHE SCHEMA CORRECTED ME WHILE I WROTE THIS. mutation/0011 was first written with url and rationale keys, and the validator refused both: a Mutation's key set is closed to implements, pullRequest, run, headSha, mutant, check, verdict and supersedes. That division is right and worth keeping -- a Mutation records what was broken, what tripped and what the verdict was; reasoning about what a verdict MEANS belongs in a Decision, where it can be argued with. The evidence link lives in the workflow run reachable from the pull request, not duplicated into the node.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:10-user-facing-surface"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/home-dashboard",
        "item/ledger-mutation-nodes",
        "item/container-smoke-ci"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0041",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T07:05:00Z",
      "run": "run/0019",
      "name": "Four CI rounds, four different findings -- and a mutation that showed the per-engine coverage is real",
      "rationale": "item/search-filters took four CI rounds and each one found something different. Recorded separately because collapsing them into \"it took four rounds\" loses three findings.\n\nROUND ONE, phpstan: five errors, none of them the one I had predicted in the pull request body. I had written that phpstan would likely object to a deliberate double assignment in Fts5SearchIndex; it never mentioned it. The five were all in PropertyFilter, and two had distinct causes. PropertyDefinition casts data_type to PropertyDataType but carried no @property annotation, so phpstan read the string column and $definition->data_type->column() looked like calling a method on a string -- DirectoryGrant already carries exactly that annotation for exactly that reason, and its own comment says the other annotated models do too; this one was simply missed. And PropertyDefinition::find() took a mixed value off a query string, which phpstan widens to Model|Collection|null because find() also accepts arrays of keys. The obvious repair there is a cast, and the cast is wrong: (int) \"abc\" is 0, which looks up definition zero and returns a silent wrong answer instead of no answer. Validated with filter_var and looked up through whereKey()->first() instead.\n\nROUND TWO, the baseline: the annotation fixed more than the file this item added. Three other call sites had been confused by the same missing annotation and BASELINED that way -- SetProperties calling cast(), includes() and rules(), Property calling cast() and column(). phpstan's baseline is strict about entries that no longer match, so the round went red for the opposite reason to the first: not new errors, but five dead ignore rules. The baseline shrinks from 40 blocks to 35, removed because the errors are gone rather than silenced. Worth noting that the strictness which reddened the round is the same strictness that turned a local fix into a measurable reduction; a lenient baseline would have kept five dead entries forever.\n\nROUND THREE, a wedge six checks downstream: image failed in checkTrashViewRestoreAndPurge's second upload with the Upload button stuck disabled for twenty seconds. The probe showed that upload completing fully -- all four requests, all 200 -- and then twenty seconds of no network activity at all. A wedged Livewire loading state, not a dropped click. main was green with the same check, so the difference was this branch, and the only thing it adds upstream is the new search-filter check, whose last action fills the year field with an empty string -- a DEBOUNCED commit -- and returns as soon as the row reappears. That proves one round trip landed, not that the component went idle. decision/0033 is precisely this shape: a check that leaves the browser mid-navigation blames its successor, and the blame lands somewhere unrelated and expensive to trace. A networkidle settle was added. This attribution is CONSISTENT with the next run passing and is not confirmed by it -- one green run does not prove an intermittent wedge gone, and it is recorded as the mechanism judged most likely rather than as a reproduction.\n\nROUND FOUR, infrastructure: docker buildx got a 502 from the registry pulling moby/buildkit while booting the builder, before the image was built or a single [setup] line printed. That is the one case the rules allow a re-run, and it was spent there. It says nothing either way about round three's fix.\n\nTHE GUARD THE WORKER CAUGHT ITSELF. Before any of that, the implementing worker reported -- rather than let pass -- that its own FTS5 mutation entry failed for the wrong reason: removing the SQL fragment \"AND d.directory_id IN (...)\" alone left the directory ids in $bindings, so the placeholder count desynced and SQLite threw. The guard would have \"failed when removed\" by proving SQLite counts placeholders, not that the predicate keeps unreachable documents out. DirectoryPolicy's entry spells out the same trap for a policy clause in as many words. It was reachable here because the predicate and its bindings were built in two separate places, so no contiguous removal could take both; they are now built together, both defaulting to empty, so removal leaves valid SQL that simply stops restricting by directory.\n\nTHE FINDING NOBODY PLANNED. The mutation dropped period_year from the FTS5 filter list, and the test matrix split exactly along the backend boundary: both sqlite jobs red, both pgsql and both mysql jobs green, because those run LikeSearchIndex which was untouched. This item's largest risk was a filter implemented in one backend and forgotten in the other -- green on one engine, broken or leaking on another. The split is incidental evidence that such a gap would surface as a per-engine failure rather than hiding behind assertions duplicated across two files that do not actually exercise different code.\n\nWHAT IS NOT PROVEN. Only the PERIOD filter has a smoke assertion shown to fail. Mime, type and property have tests including out-of-reach cases, and the two property-filter permission guards are mechanical entries that guards re-proves every run, but the container-level evidence covers period alone.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:8-search"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/search-filters",
        "item/container-smoke-ci",
        "item/ci-green-baseline"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0042",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T07:40:00Z",
      "run": "run/0019",
      "name": "A doneWhen clause that presupposes an impossible case is refused, and the invariant it rests on is enforced instead",
      "rationale": "item/login-by-username asked for something that should not be built, and the right answer was to refuse that clause and fix what it was standing on.\n\nTHE CLAUSE, AND WHY IT WAS DECLINED AS WRITTEN. The doneWhen asked for \"the collision rule decided and stated\" for a value that is a valid username AND some other account's email. Checked before briefing the worker: usernameRules() constrains usernames to /^[a-z0-9._-]+$/, which excludes @, and an email must contain one. The character sets are disjoint and the collision is unreachable by construction. Inventing a precedence rule for it would have been worse than leaving the clause unsatisfied: it would assert an ambiguity that does not exist, and the next person to read it would believe the ambiguity was real and reason from it. The docblock states the disjointness instead, and names what keeps it true.\n\nWHAT THE CLAUSE WAS ACTUALLY STANDING ON. The disjointness was held by convention, not by anything. Every application write path validates because each one remembers to; UserFactory is already a path that does not, and there is no model-level enforcement on username at all -- unlike email, whose saving() hook describes itself as \"the one hook nothing can bypass\". The implementing worker enumerated every write path as asked and reported that gap plainly rather than glossing it, which is what made the decision available.\n\nWHY IT HAD TO BE CLOSED HERE RATHER THAN FILED. AuthenticateUser now dispatches on str_contains($identifier, '@'). Before this item nothing consulted that property of a username, so an unvalidated one was harmless. After it, a username equal to another account's email sends the typing user to THAT account's row -- an account-takeover shape, not a failed login. decision/0034 records the same pattern for DirectoryPolicy::manageAccess(): a gap latent only because no caller exercised it, closed by the item that made it live rather than filed for later. User::booted() now folds the username and raises UsernameWouldBeAmbiguous on an @, beside the email fold and for the same stated reason.\n\nTHE MUTATION ENTRY, SHAPED BY THE PREVIOUS ITEM'S LESSON. user-model-refuses-an-at-sign-in-a-username removes the guard but LEAVES the fold, so the mutated code is valid PHP that persists an @ username and the test fails on data rather than on an exception. item/search-filters had to learn that the hard way one item earlier, when its FTS5 guard removed a SQL fragment and left its bindings behind, so the mutation failed on a placeholder mismatch and would have proved that SQLite counts placeholders. Applied here before CI had to teach it twice; simulated locally rather than assumed.\n\nA GUARD THAT ASKED FOR A SENTENCE AND DESERVED IT. SchemaPortabilityTest, decision/0010's audit, failed on every engine: two files under app/ now match its driver-dependent scan and were unregistered. Both are mb_strtolower() -- the scanner's pattern includes lower\\( -- and neither is raw SQL. The diff was computed locally rather than guessed: exactly two, both from this item, no stale entries. The registration says why these sites are the OPPOSITE of a portability hazard: the fold happens in PHP before any lookup, so no comparison depends on a column collation, where PostgreSQL would compare case-sensitively and SQLite and MySQL would not. The test was not an obstacle to route around; it asked for a sentence about what this code depends on, and the sentence is worth having.\n\nTHE MUTATION'S VERDICT WAS SHARPER THAN THE PREDICTION. PR 169 pinned the dispatch back to email-only and left everything else -- the folding, the model guard, and the blade's type=\"text\" field with its \"Username or email\" label. So the page still invited a username and only the server could not authenticate one, which is the shape a half-finished version of this item would have. The whole setup phase passed, INCLUDING its email logins, and only the verify phase's username login was stranded. The verdict therefore isolates username resolution rather than login at large. Reverting the blade too would have made the mutation louder and proved less, because the browser would have rejected the input before the server was ever consulted.\n\nAND THE FAILURE NAMED NOTHING, AGAIN. It arrived as a bare page.waitForURL timeout, readable only because the console line above happened to say USERNAME. Rule 3 says a failure that names no assertion is not evidence, and item/home-dashboard fixed the same shape two items ago. Named here, and named with the distinction the mutation revealed rather than generically: every earlier email login passed, so being stranded at this one is what separates a broken username branch from a broken login.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:10-user-facing-surface"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/login-by-username",
        "item/email-folding",
        "item/directory-access-ui"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0043",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T07:52:00Z",
      "run": "run/0019",
      "name": "main's push run becomes recorded evidence at merge time, and the field's limits are stated with it",
      "rationale": "mainRunUrl and mainConclusion shipped with item/ledger-mutation-nodes as vocabulary only, populated on zero nodes, and #156's own merge message says so. They are not decoration and not optional: LedgerValidator::runErrors() refuses to let a run past MUTATION_RULES_EFFECTIVE_AFTER_RUN carry outcome completed while any PullRequest merged in it lacks mainConclusion. run/0019 is the first run the rule binds on, so the run could not have been closed at all without this.\n\nPOPULATED MID-RUN, NOT AT CLOSE, AND THAT IS THE POINT. Closing the run is when the validator would have demanded these, and it is the worst moment to supply them: three merges back, the run URLs would have to be found again and matched to shas from memory, and the temptation would be to write success because main is green NOW rather than because that merge's own push run was green THEN. The field can only honestly record a verification that was actually performed at the merge it describes. All three were -- LOOP step 3 re-reads main's own push runs after every merge, filtered to head_branch main and event push -- so this change is transcription, not reconstruction. The obligation is now discharged as each merge happens.\n\nWHAT THE FIELD DOES NOT ASSERT, WRITTEN DOWN BECAUSE IT READS LIKE IT DOES. A push to main starts TWO workflows here: tests (the image build, guards, lint, pint, build assets, composer audit, dependency review and the six-engine matrix) and ledger (the validator alone). mainRunUrl is singular. It names the tests run, because that is the substantive one. So mainConclusion: success does NOT by itself state that the ledger workflow was also green on that sha. Both were checked for all three of these merges; the schema records one. A later reader must not take the field as a claim about every workflow on that commit, and the schema was not widened mid-run to make it one -- decision/0040 refused the same widening for the same reason. Turning mainRunUrl into a list of every workflow run on the merge sha is a legitimate future change; its cost is that a record is then only as complete as the caller filling it, where today the field has one stated meaning it cannot quietly under-report.\n\nTHE RULE MISSES THE ONE CLASS OF MERGE THAT HAS ACTUALLY BROKEN MAIN. PullRequest nodes exist for item PRs and mutation PRs. Ledger-only PRs -- #151, #155, #157, #159, #164, #167, #170 -- have no node at all, by consistent practice rather than by any rule. The validator's check reaches only nodes that exist, so it cannot notice a ledger merge whose main run went red. That is not hypothetical: main went red at 9e2f7af on a ledger-only merge, which is decision/0039 and PR #158, and again at 6663282 before it (PR #138). Both times the breakage was pre-existing and a ledger diff merely triggered the run that exposed it -- which is exactly why those merges are worth recording rather than exempt. Named here as a known gap rather than closed in passing: giving ledger PRs nodes changes what a PullRequest node means (it currently means a PR that implements a backlog item, and 'implements' is a required key), so it is a schema question for its own item, not a field to start filling silently.\n\nA SECOND LIMIT, FOUND WHILE WRITING THIS DOWN. The rule requires mainConclusion to be PRESENT, not to be success. MutationNodeRulesTest's own fixture proves it: the same run validates clean with mainConclusion 'failure'. That is deliberate and should stay -- main has genuinely been red after a merge here, and a schema that could not record a red merge would force the ledger to lie exactly where it matters most. But it means \"every merged PullRequest carries a mainConclusion\" is NOT \"main was green after every merge\", and the field reads like the second. The run's own description is where a red merge has to be explained; the field only guarantees the question was asked.\n\nAND THE CHECKS ON THESE FIELDS HAD NEVER RUN. LedgerValidator checks mainConclusion against GitHub's conclusion enum and mainRunUrl against a pattern pinned to turbophp/doccum. Because the fields were populated on zero nodes, neither branch had ever executed -- not against the real ledger, and not against a fixture either: MutationNodeRulesTest exercised only the presence rule, with 'success' and 'failure', both valid. Two unfalsifiable branches that read as validation. This change is the first execution of both, and it is therefore the change that owes them a test, by decision/0034's rule -- a latent gap is closed by whatever makes it live, not filed. Tests added here for a conclusion GitHub never returns ('green', which is what a hand-written value looks like) and for a well-formed Actions run URL belonging to another repository, which would read as evidence while proving nothing about this project's main branch.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/ledger-mutation-nodes",
        "item/home-dashboard",
        "item/search-filters",
        "item/login-by-username"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0044",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T08:05:00Z",
      "run": "run/0019",
      "name": "The orphan sweep ran with a broken instrument, and the consult found the field I had copied forward",
      "rationale": "LOOP step 1's sweep, plus a Fable consultation on decision/0043, and both produced a correction to my own work.\n\nTHE SWEEP'S METHOD WAS WRONG, WHICH MATTERS MORE THAN ITS RESULT. I listed open issues carrying no item by searching the ledger for the string '/issues/N'. That found five orphans. It was a false-negative scan: an issue folded into a doneWhen is named in PROSE, as '#86', not as a URL. Three of the five were already disposed of -- #86 in item/trash-view (directory purge out of v1 scope, the cascade hazard written in as a constraint the build honoured), #115 in item/api-presigned-upload (the replace entry point is REQUIRED there, because the API commit step has no Directory and no client filename), and #109 in item/api-content (404-or-403 settled as 404 for the whole product, Browser::moveFile/moveDirectory included). Only #161 and #153 were real. This is the second time a too-narrow grep nearly produced a wrong action in this project: the first was an escaped apostrophe in a test source that made a correct mutation entry look broken. A sweep run with a broken instrument is worse than no sweep, because it reports completeness. The rule going forward: match both the URL and the bare '#N' form, and across runs/ as well as ledger.jsonld.\n\nTHE TWO REAL ORPHANS. #161 is the sharper one: email verification is enabled in config, the dashboard route carries the `verified` middleware, four tests pass -- and nothing is gated, because User gets MustVerifyEmail's TRAIT from its base class while EnsureEmailIsVerified gates on the CONTRACT, which User does not declare. The instanceof is false and every unverified user walks through. The same shape as the defects HANDOVER.md records under 'a green suite is not proof', and it is not a one-line fix: a fresh container has no mailer, so implementing the contract naively locks the installer's first administrator out of their own instance. #153 is the extraction path's bare RuntimeException, the shape item/download-missing-object fixed on the download routes. Both are now items rather than notes, because LOOP step 4 picks items and nothing else.\n\nTHE CONSULT SAID decision/0043 WAS HALF RIGHT, AND IT WAS RIGHT ABOUT WHICH HALF. Not widening the schema in passing was correct. Leaving the gap as prose was not: decision/0038's own words are that prose is 'exactly where the previous one hid', and LOOP step 1 says an undisposed issue stays invisible until item/v1-audit, which has no slack. Filing an item is not a schema change and needed no deferral. The gap is now item/ledger-main-push-record.\n\nAND IT RE-DIAGNOSED THE GAP, CORRECTLY. I had it as 'ledger PRs have no node'. That is the symptom. The condition is that the ledger enumerates pushes BY PullRequest node, and no schema change closes an enumeration gap -- whatever type is added, runErrors() iterates nodes that exist, so a merge nobody wrote down stays invisible under every option I had listed. The only thing that closes it is a cross-check against an external list, and the lever was already there: ledger.yml checks out the repository at fetch-depth 1, and at depth 0 the CLI wrapper can hand the validator `git log --first-parent`. The design consultation excluded git from the VALIDATOR; it never excluded the workflow that already has the checkout. That clause is the difference between the item being a guarantee and being bookkeeping.\n\nONE CLAIM CHECKED AND REJECTED. The consult reported that decision/0043's own commit was a non-merge commit sitting directly on main, and reasoned from it that 'pushes to main are not all PR nodes' in a stronger sense than ledger PRs. It hedged appropriately, and it was wrong: 6bdfd7d was a commit on the branch behind PR #171, read while that branch existed, and it reached main as the merge a0e9db2. `git log --first-parent origin/main` shows every push to main is a merge commit. The ratio it drew the conclusion from survives anyway -- run/0019 has 8 merges and 3 carry mainConclusion -- so the finding stands and its supporting fact did not. Verified before acting, per the loop's standing instruction that this consultant is reliable on substance and not on details.\n\nA THIRD DECORATIVE FIELD, AND IT IS MINE. Run.tests and Run.assertions are copied forward rather than measured. run/0016 and run/0017 both read 649/1340 across a run that merged three items; run/0019 carried run/0018's 701/1446 while merging three more. I opened run/0019 and copied those numbers. They are set to -1 here, which the schema already permits, because a copied number reads as a measurement and is therefore worse than no number -- and they cannot be re-measured in this environment, where there is no vendor/ and the suite runs only in CI. Run.commit is the same family: no stated semantics, and run/0017's is main at that run's START rather than its work. Both go to item/ledger-field-audit, together with Decision.evidence, which is populated on 0 of 43 decisions and whose branch has never executed -- the exact state decision/0043 corrected for mainConclusion, one node type over, and which I would not have looked for had the consult not asked.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/email-verification-decided",
        "item/extraction-missing-object",
        "item/ledger-main-push-record",
        "item/ledger-field-audit",
        "item/ledger-mutation-nodes"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0045",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T08:20:00Z",
      "run": "run/0019",
      "name": "A model hook registered in the wrong boot phase is a guard that reads as protection and enforces nothing",
      "rationale": "I briefed the worker to register item/admin-users's `deleting` guard in User::booted(), beside the two saving hooks already there. It came back in booting() instead, with the reasoning written out, and the reasoning was right.\n\nWHY booted() WOULD HAVE BEEN A SILENT NO-OP. Spatie's HasRoles::bootHasRoles() registers its own `deleting` listener, and its guard clause is `method_exists($model, 'isForceDeleting') && ! $model->isForceDeleting()`. User has no SoftDeletes, so isForceDeleting() does not exist on it, the clause is false, and the listener UNCONDITIONALLY runs $model->roles()->detach(). Model::bootIfNotBooted() runs booting() then boot(), and boot() IS bootTraits() -- which is where Spatie registers. Illuminate's Dispatcher fires listeners for one event in registration order. So a listener added in booted() runs after the pivot rows it needs to read are already gone: the holder query comes back false for the actual sole administrator, and the guard never refuses anything. Every test that does not mutation-check it passes. CLAUDE.md's line is exact -- that is worse than no guard, because it looks protected.\n\nVERIFIED, NOT ACCEPTED. The worker could not run any of this (no vendor/ in this environment) and said so. I fetched both sources at the versions composer.lock pins -- spatie/laravel-permission 8.3.0 and laravel/framework v13.31.0 -- and read bootHasRoles() and bootIfNotBooted() directly before agreeing. A subagent's reasoning about framework internals it cannot execute is a hypothesis; the pinned source is the primary source.\n\nTHE GENERAL RULE. Where a model hook and a package trait both listen for the same Eloquent event, the ORDER is part of the design and has to be stated where the hook is registered. This one now carries that explanation in full, including what breaks if someone later 'tidies' it into booted() beside its siblings -- which is exactly what a reader would do, because that is where every other hook on this model lives.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:5-access-control"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/admin-users",
        "item/email-folding",
        "item/login-by-username"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0046",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T08:25:00Z",
      "run": "run/0019",
      "name": "Deleting a user before logging them out resurrects the row, and CI found it on a test this branch never touched",
      "rationale": "item/admin-users's first shape reordered DeleteUserForm to delete the account BEFORE calling $logout(), for a good reason: the sole administrator should not be logged out on the way to being told the deletion is refused. CI failed two tests on all six engines, and one of them -- test_user_can_delete_their_account in ProfileUpdateTest -- predates this work entirely and was not touched by the branch. Both said the same thing: no errors, the redirect happened, and the account was still there.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. Model::delete() ends in performDeleteOnModel(), which sets $exists = false. SessionGuard::logout() then calls cycleRememberToken() for any user whose remember_token is non-empty, and that reaches EloquentUserProvider::updateRememberToken(), which calls $user->save(). save() on a model with $exists = false is an INSERT. So deleting first and logging out second re-creates the row that was just deleted, silently. Laravel's own UserFactory sets remember_token, so every factory-made user hits it. Read from laravel/framework v13.31.0, the pinned version, rather than inferred.\n\nTHE FIX KEEPS BOTH PROPERTIES. The delete goes back after the logout, where it was. The refusal is decided before it, by asking App\\Support\\LastAdministrator directly rather than catching what delete() throws. That predicate is now the single implementation of the rule: User::booting()'s hook is still the guarantee nothing bypasses -- a console command, tinker, a future API -- and the form is the readable refusal in front of it. Two callers, one rule, rather than two rules that drift.\n\nIT ALSO REMOVED TWO PHPSTAN FINDINGS STRUCTURALLY, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. My first response to those findings was a line-level @phpstan-ignore for a dead catch, with a careful comment explaining why phpstan was wrong. The comment was accurate and the fix was still second-best: once the guard is a predicate rather than an exception to catch, there is no catch to call dead. The other finding was a real bug -- $logout($user) passed an argument to a zero-parameter __invoke, which the previous tap(Auth::user(), $logout(...)) had hidden because phpstan does not check arity through tap(). A suppression would have left that one standing next to it.\n\nWHAT THIS SAYS ABOUT THE SUITE. A pre-existing test caught a regression introduced by a change to a different concern in the same method. That is the suite doing its job, and it is also the answer to why the reordering looked safe: nothing about delete() and logout() suggests an ordering dependency until you read what logout() does to the model it was handed.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:5-access-control"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/admin-users"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0047",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T08:33:00Z",
      "run": "run/0019",
      "name": "Two sessions write to main now, and a conflict resolved by taking one side can silently drop the other's work",
      "rationale": "PR #163 -- a design system and a rebuilt file browser, from another session -- merged onto main while item/admin-users was in CI. The first symptom was not a conflict marker but an ABSENCE: the tests workflow never ran on the branch's head, because GitHub cannot build a merge commit for an un-mergeable PR and so never triggers the pull_request event. A PR that looks like it is waiting for CI may be waiting for a conflict nobody has noticed.\n\nTHE DANGEROUS CONFLICT WAS THE ONE THAT DID NOT LOOK DANGEROUS. Three files conflicted. routes/web.php and mutations.json were additive on both sides -- keep both, union the entries. topbar.blade.php was not: #163 REMOVED the Settings entry from the navbar and put it in the account dropdown, still gated on properties.manage alone. Taking main's side wholesale is the obvious resolution, it produces a file that reads correctly, CI passes -- and item/admin-users's own doneWhen work is gone, because the widening that lets a users.manage-only admin reach Settings was in the region that was deleted. The fix had to be re-applied in Settings's new home. A conflict resolution that drops a clause is indistinguishable, afterwards, from an item that never implemented it.\n\nSO THE RULE: when a conflict region on one side contains this item's OWN change, the resolution is not 'ours' or 'theirs' but 're-apply', and it is checked against the doneWhen rather than against whether the file parses. All 50 mutations.json entries were re-verified against the merged tree for the same reason -- a union is only correct if every remove string still matches its file exactly once, and half of them now point at code #163 wrote.\n\nALSO OBSERVED, AND NOT A PROBLEM: the format workflow's bot pushed `style: apply pint` onto the mutation branch, dropping the import the removed throw had used. It changed no behaviour -- the guard was already gone -- and the image job had already run on the pre-formatter sha, which is the sha mutation/0014 records. Worth knowing that an automated formatter will touch a deliberately-broken branch.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/admin-users",
        "item/ledger-main-push-record"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0048",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T09:30:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "A page nothing links to is not shipped, and route()-based tests cannot tell",
      "rationale": "item/admin-roles opened by finding that item/admin-users's page was UNREACHABLE in the product. The Settings entries in the account menu were `@can('properties.manage') ... @elsecan('users.manage')`, and the seeded admin role holds every permission, so the first branch always won for the only administrator a shipped instance has. /admin/users had no link anywhere.\n\nNothing caught it, and each reason is a pattern rather than an oversight. Every feature test reaches the page through route(). The container smoke navigates by URL. AppShellTest's own isolation of the \"users.manage but not properties.manage\" case uses a DIRECT grant, and its comment says why -- \"no admin role, which would hold properties.manage too and mask this\". The workaround that made the users-only case testable is precisely what kept the real case invisible: every existing assertion used a viewer holding exactly ONE permission, and the defect only exists for a viewer holding several.\n\nSO THE RULE, for any item that adds a page: one assertion must be made as the viewer a shipped instance actually has -- the administrator holding EVERYTHING -- and it must reach the page the way an operator does, from the shell, not from route(). Both were added here, in AppShellTest and in checkTopbar. An `@elsecan` chain between independent capabilities is now a smell in this codebase: independent permissions get independent `@can` blocks.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:10-user-facing-surface"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/admin-roles",
        "item/admin-users",
        "item/topbar-shell"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0049",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T09:30:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "The invariant, not the role named admin",
      "rationale": "item/admin-roles's doneWhen reads \"the admin role cannot lose users.manage\". It is implemented instead as: a permission change is refused when it would leave ZERO users holding users.manage anywhere in the instance.\n\nThe literal clause protects a string. Rename the role and the protection silently stops applying; create a custom role that is the only carrier of users.manage and it was never protected at all. The invariant is what the clause is trying to express, and it is the one an operator would state if asked.\n\nIt is implemented as a sibling method on App\\Support\\LastAdministrator rather than a second implementation, so the user-side rule (item/admin-users: deleting the sole holder, stripping their role) and the role-side rule (editing the role out from under every holder at once) cannot drift. Its load-bearing clause is \"some OTHER role\": the role being edited is exactly the grant being removed, so it cannot be what saves anyone from losing the permission. Its load-bearing early return is inherited too -- if nobody holds the permission at all, every role edit is PERMITTED, or an instance in that state would refuse every edit forever with no UI path left to grant it back.\n\nAlso recorded because it is one level more dangerous than the user-side case, and the code says so: no Eloquent event fires on a Spatie permission sync -- syncPermissions() writes the pivot directly, and Spatie's cache hooks bind to Role/Permission model saves, not pivot syncs. item/admin-users has a model hook behind its action as a guarantee nothing bypasses. This path has nothing behind it. The action's own correctness is the whole of the protection.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:10-user-facing-surface"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/admin-roles",
        "item/admin-users"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0050",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T09:30:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "PHPUnit --filter is a regex, and a filter that matches nothing reports as a failing assertion",
      "rationale": "The guards job refused this item with `set-role-permissions-forgets-cached-permissions: \"...\" does not pass with the guard in place`. That statement was FALSE, and believing it cost a diagnosis: the test passed perfectly well.\n\n.github/scripts/mutation-check.php shell-escapes expectFailing before passing it to `--filter`, but does not REGEX-escape it, and --filter is a regex. The test name contained `can()`; the parentheses were read as an empty capture group, so the pattern matched only the paren-less text, no test was selected, and a run that executes zero tests exits non-zero -- which the script reports in the same words it uses for an assertion that genuinely failed.\n\nTwo separate lessons. The tooling one is filed as #180 (item/mutation-filter-regex): escape it, and report \"selected no tests\" as its own outcome, because those two states mean opposite things -- one says the guard is not load-bearing, the other says the harness never looked. The discipline one is that a tool's own diagnosis is evidence, not fact: this message named the wrong cause with complete confidence, and the first fix attempted was a rewrite of a test that had nothing wrong with it.\n\nAll expectFailing values in the tree were then audited. Thirteen contain `.` or `/`, which match themselves and are harmless; none carries a character that changes what the pattern matches. The test here was renamed rather than left to depend on that audit staying true.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/admin-roles",
        "item/mutation-filter-regex"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0051",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T09:30:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "When a mutation says a guard is not load-bearing, deleting the guard can be the right answer",
      "rationale": "SetRolePermissions was written with an explicit `forgetCachedPermissions()` call after the sync, described in the design as the whole of doneWhen clause one -- \"toggling a permission changes what the Policy allows in the same request\". guards refused it: the named test STILL PASSED with that call deleted.\n\nThe reason was in the package, not in the test. Spatie\\Permission\\Traits\\HasPermissions::syncPermissions() (8.3.0) ends in $this->forgetCachedPermissions() itself. Our call was repeating work already done.\n\nThe reflex when a mutation fails this way is to strengthen the test until it detects the guard. Here that would have been wrong, because the guard was not doing anything: the honest options were to delete it or to admit the clause is satisfied by the dependency. Deleting it is also the more INFORMATIVE choice. With our own call present, the test would keep passing even if a future release stopped forgetting -- our redundancy would hide exactly the upgrade that breaks us. Without it, the test tracks the behaviour the action actually depends on and goes red on that upgrade, which is the moment someone needs to know. It is relabelled as what it is: a characterisation test over the package's contract, not a guard over our own code.\n\nCLAUDE.md already says a guard whose test passes without it is worse than none, because it looks protected. This adds the corollary: the fix is not always a better test.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/admin-roles"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0052",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T09:30:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "A dot in a wire:model path is nesting, so a permission name cannot be a path segment",
      "rationale": "The roles matrix was first bound as `wire:model=\"permissionChoice.{roleId}.{permission}\"`, one boolean per cell -- the obvious shape. Every save then failed with PermissionDoesNotExist: no permission named 'periods'.\n\nLivewire reads a dot in a property path as array nesting, and EVERY name in RolesAndPermissionsSeeder::PERMISSIONS contains one. `permissionChoice.1.periods.manage` bound ['periods']['manage'], and the component read back the key 'periods'. The binding is a list of checked names per role instead, with the permission name carried as the checkbox's `value`, where a dot is just a character.\n\nThe general rule for this codebase: a domain string that may contain a dot -- a permission name, a property key, a MIME type, a filename -- may be a wire:model VALUE but never a segment of the path. Worth recording because the failure surfaced two layers away from its cause, as a package exception naming a permission nobody wrote, and because the obvious binding is the broken one.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/admin-roles"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0053",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T09:30:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "networkidle is not a settle for a click that starts a Livewire request",
      "rationale": "An image job on this item's branch failed in checkTrashViewRestoreAndPurge() -- a check belonging to item/trash-view, on a branch whose diff touches nothing in that path: `RESTORE_LIVE:yes PURGED_GONE:no`.\n\nIt is the check, not Purge. The container access log for that run has no 4xx and no 5xx anywhere, and exactly two Livewire POSTs with Referer /trash -- both clicks were made and both were handled normally. The purge POST is logged as handled AFTER the check had already read the database and reported it still present.\n\nThe mechanism is that `waitForLoadState('networkidle')` resolves once the page has had no network activity for 500ms -- and Livewire does not issue its XHR synchronously inside the click handler. Called immediately after the click, it can find the page still idle and resolve instantly, before the request it is supposed to be settling has started. The `.catch(() => {})` means there is not even a timeout to notice. The next statement shells into the container to run tinker, which takes about a second to boot Laravel: just enough to lose the race. Restore survives only by luck, because the whole purge sequence runs after it.\n\nThis is worse than a flake: it makes the two assertions that are item/trash-view's recorded evidence intermittently meaningless. The fix (#182, item/smoke-livewire-settle) waits for the RESPONSE the click caused, registered before the click, the way setFileAndWaitForUpload() already does -- which keeps decision/0033 intact, since it waits on the request, never on the row disappearing. Two constraints carried over from run/0014: the matcher may not hard-code the endpoint, whose prefix carries a per-install hash, and it must degrade to today's behaviour rather than become the failure if Livewire renames it.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/smoke-livewire-settle",
        "item/trash-view"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0054",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T10:10:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "A rule with no witness, and a branch with no population, are one class -- and the mutation harness already solves it from the input side",
      "rationale": "Four things in two runs turned out to be assertions that looked like evidence and were not: the dead duplicate-@id loop (decision/0053's sibling, fixed in #186), mainRunUrl/mainConclusion required by a rule and populated by nobody, an explicit forgetCachedPermissions() call the package already made, and the topbar smoke pair CLAUDE.md records. Each was found by a different accident, which is not a coincidence.\n\nA consult (fable) split them, and the split is the useful part. A RULE WITH NO WITNESS (the dead loop; the topbar half) is a check nothing has ever fed an input that trips it. A BRANCH WITH NO POPULATION (mainRunUrl before run 19; Decision.evidence, 0 of 53 today) is code consuming a field no node carries. A COPIED VALUE (Run.tests, Run.assertions) is detectable by no mutant at all -- only by provenance or deletion. And SetRolePermissions was not an instance: guards refused a redundant guard, which is the property WORKING. Three of four, not four, and counting the fourth overstated the problem while understating the tool already in the repo.\n\nThe property that covers the first two is the one mutation-check.php has, with the mutant moved to the INPUT side: every rule has a witness that trips it, and every error-emission site is reached by at least one witness, both asserted in CI. For application code the mutant deletes a guard and a named test must fail. For the validator the mutant is a JSON-level edit to a copy of the LIVE ledger, and the flip is: the unmutated copy validates clean, the mutated copy yields at least one error of the expected code. The census clause is what catches the dead loop -- a site no witness reaches becomes a COUNT rather than an accident three runs later.\n\nFiled as #189 (the ratchet) and #188 (the immediate hole: ~64 is_string sites that SKIP a wrong-shaped value instead of failing it -- decision/0030.supersedes is an array where context.jsonld declares a single @id and validates clean, and a bare-string implements crashes the validator with a TypeError rather than reporting). The vocabulary verdicts went to #173. Every factual claim here was re-verified against the code before filing; the consult was also right that its own premise check mattered -- it read main before #186 merged and said so.\n\nDeliberately NOT applied: per-PR image mutation of smoke checks. One build plus smoke per mutant is ten to thirty minutes, and the one-time proof already lives in the Mutation nodes. The gap there is DECAY -- the topbar half was load-bearing once and stopped being -- which wants a periodic replay, not a per-PR gate.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/ledger-rule-witnesses",
        "item/ledger-shape-from-context",
        "item/ledger-field-audit",
        "item/ledger-id-uniqueness"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0055",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T10:10:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "order is a plan, so a v1 item may not sit after the tag that depends on it",
      "rationale": "item/mutation-filter-regex and item/smoke-livewire-settle were appended at max(order)+1, giving them 120 and 121 -- release v1.0.0, but ordered after item/tag-v1-0-0 (58) and after sixteen post-v1 items. That was my own edit in run/0020 and I did not check. Read as a plan it says 'tag v1, then do two things that block tagging v1', and the validator's own rule (the tag cannot complete until every v1.0.0 item is Completed) makes that a contradiction rather than merely untidy.\n\nThe repair: 43 and 44 fell vacant when #186 removed the two duplicate items, and the v1 tail (45-58) shifts up by three, opening 45-47. The five v1 items that needed a home -- the two misplaced ones and the three new ledger items -- now all sit before item/v1-audit and item/tag-v1-0-0.\n\nWhat order does NOT carry: contiguity. It is already sparse (a gap after 58, then a jump to 100+), no rule reads a gap, and no application code reads order at all. A gap is an honest record that something was removed. What it DOES carry is one invariant worth enforcing rather than remembering: every v1.0.0 item's order < item/v1-audit < item/tag-v1-0-0 < every post-v1 item. That belongs in the validator, and is folded into #173.\n\nRenumbering is not a history rewrite. LOOP.md's append-only clause covers runs and decisions -- what happened. order is what is planned next, and a plan that has stopped describing the intended sequence should be corrected rather than preserved.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/ledger-field-audit",
        "item/mutation-filter-regex",
        "item/smoke-livewire-settle"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0056",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T10:10:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "Only flux:button is known to forward an arbitrary attribute, so a flux:input is located by its label",
      "rationale": "item/admin-periods's brief told the worker to put data-test on the close-period form's inputs. It declined, and was right: flux:input renders a label and wrapper around the real <input>, and this codebase has no precedent for an attribute placed on flux:input reaching that inner element in the BUILT image -- only flux:button is proven to forward them (resources/views/livewire/files/browser.blade.php's replace-form note records where that was established). Every existing admin page's smoke locates a flux:input by getByLabel().\n\nThe cost of getting this wrong is not a visible error: a locator that matches nothing fails as a timeout attributed to the feature, three minutes into an image build, and the temptation is then to blame the feature rather than the locator. So the convention is worth stating rather than rediscovering: data-test belongs on flux:button and on plain native elements this codebase writes itself (the roles matrix's <input type=checkbox>, the trash table's rows); a flux:input is reached by its label text.\n\nRecorded also because it is the second time in two items that a worker departed from my brief and was correct -- decision/0045 was the first. A brief is a hypothesis about code the worker is about to read more closely than I did.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:10-user-facing-surface"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/admin-periods"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0057",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T11:25:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "A setting whose reader bypasses the seam is a control that governs nothing",
      "rationale": "item/admin-instance-settings's doneWhen says all writes go through the Settings service. Read as a UI instruction that is easy and worthless. retention.purge_after_years and retention.auto_purge lived in config/doccum.php OUTSIDE the settings block, and their readers used config() directly -- so a retention control could write a perfectly good settings row and change nothing at all. The purge plan would keep answering from the code default and the operator would have a governance control that governs nothing.\\n\\nBoth keys moved under config('doccum.settings') and both readers now go through Settings::get(). The second reader was not in the brief: App\\Console\\Commands\\PurgeExpired had the identical bug, and the worker found it. That was the more dangerous half -- the settings page would have appeared to work while the SCHEDULED weekly purge kept reading the code default, deleting or not deleting on the wrong rule, with nobody watching.\\n\\nThe assertion that makes the clause mean something is on BEHAVIOUR, not storage: writing retention.purge_after_years through the page changes what PeriodPurger::plan() reports in its blockers. A test asserting the stored row would be the same defect wearing a green test -- the exact shape decision/0054 catalogues.\\n\\nSO THE RULE, for any item that exposes a setting: find the code that READS it before adding the control. If nothing reads it, either wire it up or leave it out. 'limits' is named in issue #21's title and is deliberately absent, because nothing in the codebase reads an upload-size or quota setting; a limits field would have been this same defect one row over. instance.name is exposed with a flag rather than silently: it round-trips through the service and FirstRun already writes it, but nothing DISPLAYS it yet -- the topbar wordmark is a hardcoded literal. That is not the retention case (no second reader bypasses the seam) but it is not yet visible in the product either, and saying so is cheaper than someone discovering it.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:4-data-model"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/admin-instance-settings",
        "item/admin-periods"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0058",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T11:25:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "auth()->id() is int|string|null: narrow it, never cast it",
      "rationale": "phpstan refused four calls in InstanceSettings: Settings::set() takes ?int $userId and auth()->id() is int|string|null, because Laravel supports a string primary key. CLAUDE.md allows no new findings, so this had to be fixed rather than suppressed.\\n\\nThe obvious fix is (int) auth()->id(), and it is wrong twice over. It asserts something the framework does not promise -- this app's users table is bigIncrements TODAY. And a cast of a string key yields 0, which writes a settings row whose updated_by foreign key points at a user that does not exist: a silently corrupt row rather than a loud failure.\\n\\nactorId() narrows instead -- is_int($id) ? $id : null -- and the column is nullable precisely so 'unattributed' is expressible. Attribute the write when the key is what settings.updated_by can hold; leave it unattributed when it is not.\\n\\nSame reasoning that widened App\\Livewire\\Admin\\Roles::$permissionChoice's key to int|string rather than casting it (run/0020, item/admin-roles). Recorded as a pair because the general rule is one rule: when a framework type is wider than yours, move YOUR type or narrow the value -- never cast to what happens to be true in this schema this week.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/admin-instance-settings",
        "item/admin-roles"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0059",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T11:25:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "A pull request with zero check runs is conflicted until proven otherwise",
      "rationale": "decision/0047 recorded that GitHub cannot build a merge commit for an un-mergeable pull request, so the pull_request event never fires and no workflow runs. It has now happened twice, and the second time it cost more than the first.\\n\\nPR #176, from the other session writing to this repository, sat for two and a half hours with `total_count: 0` check runs -- not red, not pending, NONE -- while main moved eleven commits underneath it. Its author had every reason to read that as 'waiting for CI'. Merging main into it (a merge commit, never a rebase, so the other session's checkout stays valid) made it buildable; CI then ran for the first time and immediately found a real defect that had been invisible the whole time: the branch's new click-to-open preview modal intercepts pointer events over the detail panel, so a PRE-EXISTING smoke check could no longer click the trash button it had always clicked.\\n\\nSO THE CHECK, every loop iteration: an open PR reporting zero check runs is conflicted until proven otherwise. Red CI is visible and gets attention; ABSENT CI looks exactly like patience. mergeable_state also reads 'unknown' in this state rather than 'dirty', so the API's own answer does not distinguish it -- a local `git merge-tree` against the base does, and takes seconds.\\n\\nOn resolution: the conflict was .github/mutations.json again, both sides appending to one array, resolved as a union with all 55 entries re-verified against the merged tree -- the same shape and the same verification decision/0047 required. container-smoke.mjs and routes/web.php auto-merged. The defect CI then found was NOT pushed as a fix: it is another session's PR and the two available fixes are not equivalent (dismiss the modal in the check, or stop a plain row click from opening a preview at all), so the diagnosis went to the author as a comment and the design call stayed theirs.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/ledger-main-push-record"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0060",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T12:10:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "An operator vouches, or you prove your own address",
      "rationale": "EnsureEmailIsVerified had gated nothing since the project started. app/Models/User.php carried `// use Illuminate\\Contracts\\Auth\\MustVerifyEmail;` -- commented out -- and declared `implements PasskeyUser` alone. Authenticatable supplies the TRAIT; the middleware gates on the CONTRACT. The instanceof was false for every user who has ever existed, while config/fortify.php enabled the feature, two route groups carried `verified`, and four tests passed.\\n\\nissue #161 allowed either route: implement it or remove it. Implemented, because item/admin-instance-settings had just made public signup a real toggle, which is precisely the case verification exists for. But it could not be a one-line uncomment: NOTHING set email_verified_at outside UserFactory, and MAIL_MAILER defaults to 'log', so turning on the contract alone strands the installer's first administrator behind `verified` with the only link in the container log. A fresh container would brick on first boot.\\n\\nHence the rule: an account an operator VOUCHED for is verified at creation (the installer, because they proved control by installing it; a user created through the admin page, because a holder of users.manage vouched for the address), and a SELF-REGISTERED account proves its own. Plus doccum:user:verify, without which an operator who changes their own email -- Profile correctly nulls the column -- is locked out with no recovery but grepping a log.\\n\\nTWO BRIEFS OF MINE WERE WRONG AND THE WORKER CAUGHT BOTH, which is the part worth recording. First, I said to set email_verified_at in CreateUser::handle() 'the same as FirstRun': that action is what Fortify's CreateNewUser DELEGATES to, so taken literally it would have verified every self-registered user and destroyed the item. It takes an explicit $verified parameter instead. Second, I asserted that RegistrationTest's 'redirects to files.browse' assertions would have to change once that route was gated: they do not. RegisterResponse computes redirect()->intended() from config('fortify.home'), url.intended is only set when a request first bounces off a gated route, and assertRedirect does not follow -- middleware on a destination does not alter the Location header of a redirect to it. Rewriting those assertions would have been damage to tests that were already true. A brief is a hypothesis about code the worker is about to read more closely than I did; decision/0056 said this once already and it held twice more in one item.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:10-user-facing-surface"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/email-verification-decided",
        "item/admin-instance-settings"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0061",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T12:10:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "markEmailAsVerified() writes the column and nothing else -- the Verified event is the caller's job",
      "rationale": "CI failed one test of 832 on the first push: doccum:user:verify did not dispatch Illuminate\\Auth\\Events\\Verified. Laravel's MustVerifyEmail trait is a forceFill()->save() and no more; the event is dispatched by the HTTP flow, in Fortify's VerifyEmailController, AFTER calling it.\\n\\nSo an account verified from a console was silently DIFFERENT from one verified through a link: any listener on Verified would run for the second and not the first. The tell was already in the command -- its 'already verified' branch deliberately avoids RE-firing the event, guarding against something that never happened.\\n\\nThe test asserting the event was right and the command was wrong, so the command changed. Recorded because the opposite move -- deleting the assertion to make the suite green -- would have been available, cheap, and exactly the failure mode this whole item exists to correct.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/email-verification-decided"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0062",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T12:10:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "An assertion that cannot fail before an earlier step does is not evidence",
      "rationale": "mutation/0018 came back VOID. checkFirstAdminIsVerified() was added to the smoke to give the installer's verification write a named assertion. Removing that write turned the image job red -- but in the installer helper, with `page.waitForURL: Timeout 15000ms exceeded`, before the named check ever ran. FirstRun::submit() redirects to /files, which THIS SAME ITEM put behind `verified`; unverified, the redirect bounces and the helper times out.\\n\\nNo better mutant exists. Any change leaving the administrator unverified breaks that redirect FIRST, by construction. The check cannot fail before the installer step does, so it is unprovable as written, and CLAUDE.md's rule -- never cite an unmutated assertion as evidence -- means it counts for nothing today.\\n\\nTHE GENERAL FORM, which is the reason this is a decision and not just a note: an assertion placed downstream of a step that fails for the same cause can never be the thing that reports that cause. It is the same family as a guard that cannot fire (#185) and a control that only hides a button (decision/0051, decision/0057) -- something that looks like coverage and is not. When adding a check, ask what ELSE breaks when its subject breaks, and whether that something breaks EARLIER.\\n\\nThe fix inverts the design rather than moving the check: the smoke should report where the browser actually LANDED, so the cause is in the error text instead of inferred -- and then the separate check is genuinely redundant and goes. Filed as issue #197 / item/smoke-installer-names-the-bounce.\n\nTHE SECOND MUTATION WIDENED THAT. mutation/0019 removed `verified: true` from the admin creation path instead, and landed on checkAdminUsersPage()'s named 403 assertion -- negative, which is what finally let this item be recorded Completed. But its message was \"/admin/users answered HTTP 200 for <member>, who holds no users.manage\", which reads exactly like an authorisation bypass. It is not one: page.goto() follows redirects, so 200 is the verification notice rendering after a 302, verified by reading the check rather than assumed. A smoke message that would make a reader announce a security hole they do not have is worse than a vague one, so #197 now covers both sites through one shared helper that reports status AND landing URL.\\n\\nAlso recorded: BOTH predictions written into the mutation PR before the run were wrong -- the failure location, and 'every other job stays green' (FirstRunSetupTest caught it too). The prediction was written down first precisely so being wrong would be visible rather than quietly reinterpreted afterwards.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/email-verification-decided",
        "item/smoke-installer-names-the-bounce"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0063",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T13:20:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "A mutation that kills the test before its assertion runs proves the wrong thing",
      "rationale": "Item 43's first mutation entry, as delegated, removed `return preg_quote($expectFailing);` from buildFilterPattern(). That leaves a `: string` function returning nothing, so the mutation raised a TypeError -- the named test went red, and the entry would have passed the guards job.\n\nIt proves the wrong proposition. A TypeError means the LINE IS REQUIRED FOR THE FUNCTION TO RETURN AT ALL; it says nothing about whether the test checks the escaping, because the test dies before its assertion executes. Swap the assertion for `expect(true)->toBeTrue()` and the mutation still goes red. That is decision/0035's rule -- a red job alone is void, the failure must land AT the named assertion -- reached from a new direction: previously via a smoke check that collapsed upstream, here via a fatal inside the function under test.\n\nTHE RULE, and it is a design constraint on the code, not only on the manifest: when a line is going to be mutation-checked, write the surrounding code so that DELETING it degrades behaviour rather than breaking the contract. buildFilterPattern() is now a reassignment -- `$pattern = $expectFailing;` then `$pattern = preg_quote($pattern);` -- which looks mildly redundant and carries a comment saying why. Deleting the escape leaves a function that still returns a string, the unescaped one, and the failure lands on `expect(preg_match(...))->toBe(1)`. The redundancy is the point and is cheaper than the false confidence.\n\nAll four of item 43's mutations were then simulated locally before pushing -- apply the `remove` string, `php -l`, call the function, compare against the unmutated value -- which is how this was caught at review rather than after a green guards job had blessed it.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/mutation-filter-regex"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0064",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T13:22:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "Only a test that ran and failed is mutation proof, and the evidence for that must be machine-readable",
      "rationale": "Before item 43, mutation-check.php accepted ANY non-pass from the deleted-guard run as proof the guard was load-bearing. So a `remove` string whose deletion broke the file's syntax scored as verified: nothing was collected, the run was red, the entry read as proven, and the guard had never been exercised. The script written to catch \"looks like coverage and is not\" contained an instance of it.\n\nThree outcomes are now distinguished from evidence rather than from Pest's prose. `--log-junit=<path>` is written per run and <testcase> elements are counted: zero means --filter matched nothing (a manifest/name error, #180's original bug), a positive count with a non-zero exit means a real test ran and failed, and NO LOG AT ALL means neither -- it is no evidence, reported as unclassifiable with a message saying to fix --log-junit rather than to go renaming entries. That third state exists because conflating it with \"matched no test\" would have blamed every entry in the manifest for names each one spells correctly, the moment the logging broke: #180's misdiagnosis in a new costume. Only the second outcome now counts as proof.\n\nTHE RISK WAS REAL AND IS NOW MEASURED. Tightening this applies to every entry at once, and there was no way to run the harness locally (no vendor/, composer install blocked by egress policy), so the PR said plainly that a pre-existing entry resting on a collection failure would go red and name itself, and that such a red would be a finding to fix rather than grounds to loosen the rule back. The answer came from CI: guards passed on 7df3e10 in 72 seconds across all 60 entries. No entry was resting on a collection failure, and --log-junit works under this Pest version -- had it not, all 60 would have reported unclassifiable. Recorded because it is a measurement, not a reassurance: the tightening cost nothing and the manifest was already sound by the stricter standard.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/mutation-filter-regex",
        "item/extraction-missing-object"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0065",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T14:15:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "A rule that requires the impossible is a deadlock, not strictness",
      "rationale": "item/ledger-main-push-record's history check, as first written, required EVERY commit in the git-log window to appear in some Run.merges, all the way to the tip. It passed review and passed CI, because the backfill happened to end exactly at the main it was computed from.\n\nIt could not have survived its own merge. A merge commit's sha cannot appear in the ledger that merge commit CONTAINS -- the sha does not exist until the merge happens -- so on the push event, where MAIN_SHA is github.sha, `validate ledger` would have gone red on main the instant any PR merged. And the ledger pass that recorded those merges would create another unrecorded merge commit in turn: permanently red, with no sequence of correct actions that clears it.\n\nCaught by running the wrapper against a sha absent from Run.merges rather than by reading it, which is the only reason it was caught before merging: every local check passed, because every local check used the one sha for which the rule was satisfiable.\n\nTHE RULE, which generalises past this check: before shipping an invariant, ask what the state of the world is immediately AFTER the action the invariant governs, not only before it. An invariant that the system's own normal operation violates is not strict, it is a deadlock, and the difference is invisible while the world happens to be in the one state that satisfies it.\n\nThe fix is contiguity, not leniency: a commit BEFORE the newest recorded merge that is not itself recorded is a hole somebody left -- exactly the ledger-only merge nobody wrote down, which is the class both of main's red episodes belonged to -- while a commit AFTER it is simply not recorded yet, and becomes interior, and so caught, the moment a later merge is recorded. Verified all three ways before merging (interior hole -> reported, trailing -> not, all recorded -> not), and then verified in production: main's own run at a2e637d printed \"Compared 15 commits ... Ledger is sound\", the fifteenth being the merge commit itself.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/ledger-main-push-record"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0066",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T14:16:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "Grandfather to the last real violation, not to the newest run",
      "rationale": "MERGES_OUTCOME_RULE_EFFECTIVE_AFTER_RUN was first set to 20 -- the newest run in existence -- so that every already-closed run was exempt 'by construction'. That reads as prudent and is nearly worthless: computed over the backfill, exactly TWO runs close on a red merge, run/0000 and run/0008. A threshold of 8 grandfathers precisely those and binds runs 9 onward, twelve runs of real closed history. A threshold of 20 bound nothing at all, since run/0020 is active and the rule only applies to completed runs.\n\nThe difference was not theoretical and is worth recording because of how it surfaced. The mutation probe for that rule breaks run/0019 and checks the validator reports it; at threshold 20 it reports zero errors both mutated AND unmutated. The mutation would have been VOID while reading as proof -- the same failure decision/0062 and decision/0063 name, arrived at from a third direction: not an assertion that cannot fail, nor a mutation that fails for the wrong reason, but a rule whose scope excludes everything it could have been tested against.\n\nTHE RULE: a grandfathering threshold is a claim about history, so compute it from history. Set it to the last case that actually violates, and if that number is far below the newest run, the gap is the amount of real evidence the rule is now checked against. Setting it to 'now' exempts everything and cannot be distinguished from a rule that does not work.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/ledger-main-push-record"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0067",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T14:40:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "An assertion that the corpus is clean cannot witness the check that cleans it",
      "rationale": "item/ledger-field-audit deletes Run.tests and Run.assertions, and its doneWhen justified adding no test for them: \"a field that is DELETED needs no test, since its absence from ALLOWED_KEYS is the assertion and LedgerIntegrityTest already fails an unknown key.\"\n\nThe first half is right. The second was not true of the check as written. LedgerIntegrityTest's vocabulary test is `expect(ledgerErrorsTagged('structural:'))->toBe([])` -- an assertion that the REAL ledger currently has no structural errors. It does catch a re-added key, because the real ledger would then carry one. But it passes just as happily if the structural check is deleted outright, because an empty error list is exactly what a deleted check returns. Two field deletions were resting on a guard with no witness.\n\nTHE GENERAL FORM, and the reason this is a decision rather than a note on that item: `expect(errors)->toBe([])` over real data is a REGRESSION CHECK ON THE DATA, not a witness for the checker. Both are worth having and they are not substitutes. The corpus assertion says \"nothing in the ledger is broken today\"; only a fixture that VIOLATES the rule can say \"and the thing that would notice still works\". A codebase that only ever asserts its real data is clean cannot distinguish a clean corpus from an absent check -- which is the same inability, in a new place, that #185 (a guard that could not fire) and decision/0062 (an assertion downstream of the failure) each recorded.\n\nA second lesson, cheaper to state and easy to get wrong: the branch that catches a given violation is not always the one the prose names. tests and assertions were removed from context.jsonld as well, so re-adding `tests` trips the UNDEFINED-TERM check and continues, never reaching the per-type ALLOWED_KEYS check the doneWhen cites. The first witness written here used `tests` against the ALLOWED_KEYS branch and returned an identical message mutated and unmutated -- it would have failed even unmutated, and its mutation would have proved nothing. Found by simulating, not by reading. There are now two witnesses, one per branch, because the branches fail independently.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/ledger-field-audit"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0068",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T14:50:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "An item whose doneWhen outlived its subject is restated before it is worked, not while",
      "rationale": "item/ledger-shape-from-context (order 46, issue #188) has six clauses. One of them -- \"a mainConclusion with a null mainRunUrl is a pr: error\" -- became unsatisfiable when item/ledger-main-push-record deleted both fields from PullRequest and moved that fact into Run.merges. No PullRequest node carries them, context.jsonld no longer defines them, and the validator mentions them only in historical comments.\n\nThe loop's own rule covers this: if a doneWhen cannot be satisfied as written, say so and split or restate the item -- do not fake the clause. What it does not say, and what this decision adds, is WHEN. Restating it as part of the ledger pass that marks the item Completed would produce a record in which the requirement and the implementation match perfectly, because the requirement was edited to fit what was built. That is indistinguishable, afterwards, from having met the original clause. So the amendment lands FIRST, on its own, before the work is delegated.\n\nThe clause is restated rather than deleted, because its intent survived its subject: a conclusion recorded without the run that produced it is incoherent, and that shape simply lives on Run.merges now. Verified it is real work rather than already done -- checkMergeEntry() validates mergeSha, pullRequest, the two conclusions and the two run URLs each independently, so an entry may today claim testsConclusion 'success' with testsRun null and validate clean.\n\nGeneral form: an item's doneWhen is a claim made BEFORE the work, and its value comes entirely from having been written before. Editing one is sometimes necessary and is never free -- do it in its own change, with the reason recorded, so the edit is visible as an edit.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/ledger-shape-from-context"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0069",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T15:20:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "An over-strict rule with a named exception is acceptable where a silent skip is not",
      "rationale": "item/ledger-shape-from-context derives each term's shape from context.jsonld rather than from a hand-maintained list -- that is the item's whole point. It ships with one hand-maintained exception anyway: SHAPE_EXEMPT_TERMS = ['about'], because Ledger.about embeds a full SoftwareApplication node while the context declares it @type: @id.\n\nThe exemption is correct in substance. In JSON-LD, @type: @id coerces STRING values to IRIs; it does not forbid an embedded node object, which is legal under any term. So the shape derived for `about` is over-strict, and the data is fine.\n\nIt was still worth stopping over, in a run where five decisions have been about rules that look like they work and do not. The reason it passes: the exemption is keyed on the TERM'S NAME, so it will not generalise -- a second @id term that ever embeds a node will be wrongly reported. But that failure is LOUD. An over-strict rule reports something that is fine; it does not stay quiet about something that is broken. That asymmetry is the whole difference between this and the defect the item removes, where array_filter(..., 'is_string') dropped a malformed value and said nothing.\n\nTHE RULE: judge a shortcut by which direction it fails. A check that is too strict costs a false alarm and announces itself the first time it is wrong. A check that is too lenient costs nothing today and is indistinguishable from a working check for as long as nobody looks. Prefer the first, ship it with the exception named in one place, and revisit when a second case arrives -- rather than weakening the rule pre-emptively to accommodate a case that does not exist yet.\n\nRecorded here rather than left in PR #208's description so the next person meets the reasoning where they would meet the exemption, and can disagree with it.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/ledger-shape-from-context"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0070",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T15:25:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "The main-push-history rule caught its author, three hours after it shipped",
      "rationale": "item/ledger-main-push-record was justified on a specific claim: runErrors() iterated `pullRequests`, so a LEDGER-ONLY merge -- which by consistent practice carries no PullRequest node -- was invisible to every rule the validator had, and both times main had gone red it was exactly that class.\n\nWriting THIS pass, the rule fired:\n\n  history: commit 'c2dac7f9...' is on main but does not appear in any\n  run's merges, and a LATER commit does -- so this is a merge nobody\n  wrote down, not one not yet recorded.\n\nc2dac7f is the merge of PR #206, the ledger pass for item 42. It was trailing and legitimately unrecorded when it landed. The next pass -- this one -- should have recorded it, and instead jumped straight to the two newer merges. A ledger-only merge, omitted by the person who had just built the check for omitted ledger-only merges, within three hours of shipping it.\n\nWorth recording for three reasons.\n\nFirst, it is the difference between a rule tested against a fixture and a rule tested against the world. The witness tests for this rule pass an interior hole in deliberately. This was an accident, in real data, found by the check rather than by anyone re-reading the file.\n\nSecond, it validates the CONTIGUITY design from decision/0065 specifically. Under the original to-the-tip rule this omission could never have been distinguished from the ordinary state of a just-merged commit, because every pass has an unrecorded tip. Contiguity is what made \"not yet recorded\" and \"never recorded\" different things, and this is the first case where the difference mattered.\n\nThird, it is evidence about the author, not just the code: the omission happened while writing a ledger pass by hand, in a session whose entire subject has been checks that do not check. The habit is not the safeguard. The check is.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/ledger-main-push-record",
        "item/ledger-shape-from-context"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0071",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T16:10:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "phpstan found the ratchet in item 47 could not fire",
      "rationale": "item/ledger-rule-witnesses exists to make a rule that cannot fire visible. It shipped, in its first pushed form, containing one.\n\nThe harness carried UNWITNESSED_BASELINE (the codes with no witness) and UNWITNESSED_BASELINE_MAX (a ceiling on that list's size), and run() compared them so a baseline growing past the ceiling would fail. Both are compile-time constants and both were 0, so phpstan rejected it outright: \"Comparison operation > between 0 and 0 is always false\".\n\nEvery human step missed it. The brief named this exact trap -- a baseline is where coverage rots, enforce the shrink mechanically. The worker built the ceiling for that reason and reported it as the mechanism. I reviewed it, verified the coverage equality, spot-checked four mutations, and approved. A static analyser caught what three careful passes did not.\n\nRemoved, not suppressed: a decorative guard is worse than none, which is this item's own thesis turned on itself. What holds the line now is a Pest assertion that the baseline is empty -- a tripwire on the SOURCE rather than runtime state, which is the honest shape at the floor, since the baseline starts empty and has nowhere to ratchet down to. The only event worth detecting is somebody adding an entry, and that now means editing an assertion in the same diff a reviewer reads.\n\nTHE LESSON, which is not 'run phpstan' -- CI already did. It is that a guard comparing two things the author controls at the same moment is not a guard. The ceiling and the list were written in one commit by one author with one intent; nothing about that arrangement could ever disagree with itself. A check earns its name by being able to disagree with the thing it checks.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/ledger-rule-witnesses"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0072",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T16:12:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "An auto-formatter and a manifest of exact source strings are on a quiet collision course",
      "rationale": ".github/mutations.json identifies each guard by an exact substring of its source file, and mutation-check.php requires that substring to match exactly once. The repository also runs a pint workflow that reformats code and pushes the result onto the branch, unattended.\n\nThose two facts are in tension by construction. A formatter that changes a quote style, a unary-operator space or a trailing comma on a line some entry targets breaks the match -- and the failure does not say so. It surfaces as \"matched no test at all\", which since decision/0064 means the entry is reported as unproven rather than silently passing, but still points a reader at the manifest's NAME rather than at the reformat that actually moved the line.\n\nIt happened twice in one run: pint pushed onto item 46's branch and onto item 47's. Both times the fix was to re-verify all entries, and both times all of them still matched -- pint had touched lines nothing targeted. That is luck, not design, and worth recording BEFORE it is not luck.\n\nTHE PRACTICE: after any push that was not yours -- a formatter's, a merge from main, anybody's -- re-check that every mutations.json remove string still matches exactly once before reading CI as meaningful. It costs one command. The exact-once rule is what makes the collision loud instead of silent, and it only helps if somebody runs it at the moment the collision can happen.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:14-testing"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/mutation-filter-regex",
        "item/ledger-rule-witnesses"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0073",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T17:00:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "Ensure means create what is absent, never assert what a set must be",
      "rationale": "RolesAndPermissionsSeeder ran on EVERY container boot and ended in syncPermissions() on both roles. Its own docblock called that 'idempotent by construction -- safe on every boot'. It was idempotent against the seeder's own constants and destructive against the database: every restart silently discarded whatever an operator had configured through Settings -> Roles (issue #214).\n\nTHE RULE, which generalises well past this seeder: code that runs unattended on every boot against a database a human can edit may CREATE what is absent, and may not ASSERT what a set must be. Those are different operations and only the first is safe there. 'Ensure' reads like the second and is almost always meant as the first.\n\nThe test of whether you have the right one is not the function name but the diff it would produce against a database somebody has touched. syncPermissions() produces a deletion. findOrCreate plus a wasRecentlyCreated guard produces nothing.\n\nWhat this gives up is stated in the item and in #216 rather than discovered later: a permission added by an upgrade is no longer granted to existing roles, only created as an assignable row. That was chosen deliberately -- a capability that grants ITSELF to every existing member on upgrade is a privilege-escalation path, not a convenience -- and the cost, a release note naming any new permission, is assigned to item/changelog-release-notes.\n\nWorth promoting into CLAUDE.md alongside the seam rules: the repository has other boot-time work (docker/entrypoint.d) and the same mistake is available to all of it.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:5-access-control"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/role-permissions-survive-restart",
        "item/upgrade-preserves-access",
        "item/changelog-release-notes"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0074",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T17:02:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "The worker brief is the defect, not the decision vocabulary -- adopt promotion, reject the type split and the cap",
      "rationale": "The ledger holds 72 decisions. LOOP.md step 5 hands a worker 'its plan, the relevant spec section and CLAUDE.md'. Decisions are not in that brief, so the hardest-won rules in the project reach nobody who needs them. Four remedies were considered -- a promotion rule, splitting Decision into Decision and Finding, a retirement status, a per-run cap -- and consultation returned a diagnosis worth recording over any of them: the graph cannot even route decisions to a brief, because affects points BACKWARD at the items a decision came from. Of the open v1 items, 14 have no affecting decision at all, including every release item. A brief built from affects would be empty exactly where it is needed.\n\nMeanwhile promotion already happens and is unrecorded in both directions: CLAUDE.md's 'never cite an unmutated assertion as evidence' is decision/0012's rule with no citation, and LOOP.md step 3 is decisions 0016 and 0021 nearly verbatim, uncited. The loop has a promotion PRACTICE and no promotion RULE, so the ledger cannot say which of its 72 nodes have already been acted on.\n\nADOPTED: promotion, in two layers. Process first, at zero schema cost -- LOOP.md step 5 includes every non-superseded decision whose affects names the item, plus the promoted rules in CLAUDE.md; step 7 asks of each new decision whether it goes to CLAUDE.md, to LOOP.md, into the validator, or stays. The promoted line carries the decision id; the rationale stays here. Record second, as an S item: a promotedTo field on Decision, backfilled null.\n\nREJECTED: splitting Decision into Decision and Finding. It touches eight or more sites in a 2,000-line validator plus a new collection and new witnesses, retyping 72 existing nodes is a history rewrite, leaving them untyped yields a corpus where the split means nothing, and no consumer cares -- a worker does not need to know whether a rule was chosen or discovered, only whether it binds them. The classification is fuzzy anyway: decision/0058 is both.\n\nREJECTED as schema, ADOPTED as a read rule: retirement. supersedes already exists and is used. A superseded decision is simply never cited in a brief and never promoted. One line in LOOP.md, no validator change.\n\nREJECTED: a per-run cap. Runs are sessions of wildly different length -- run/0020 wrote roughly one decision per merge, which is the right ratio, not excess. A cap would suppress recording in long runs and bind nothing in short ones. The write-time triage a cap is meant to force is the promotion question itself, which asks 'does this generalise, and to whom' rather than 'have we hit five'.\n\nTHE FALSIFIER, fixed now so it cannot be argued later: within two runs, a worker PR description cites a decision id. If none does, promotedTo is decorative and comes out, the way decision/0044 removed tests and assertions.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:14-testing"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/ledger-field-audit",
        "item/ledger-rule-witnesses"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0075",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T17:03:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "Release mechanics move ahead of the API chain, against step 4's own tiebreak",
      "rationale": "This is a deliberate correction of the plan under decision/0055, not rule-following, and is recorded because the loop's own rule would NOT have picked it. Step 4's tiebreak is 'unblocks the most items', and by that measure item/api-sanctum-tokens wins: its chain runs eight items deep, longer than the release chain.\n\nThe facts that override it. git tag is empty -- release.yml HAS NEVER RUN. The loop's stopping condition IS that pipeline: a tag pushed, ghcr carrying the image. Leaving it at position 58 of 66, behind roughly four M and L items, means it runs for the first time within a couple of items of the v1 tag -- the exact no-slack shape LOOP.md step 1 already condemns for item/v1-audit. item/release-v0-1-0 exists precisely to exercise the pipeline early, and its position defeated its own purpose.\n\nThere is also a live defect that does not wait on any plan: release.yml has no needs on a test job and fires on workflow_dispatch, so a dispatch against a red main publishes latest. That is a violation of 'CI is the gate' sitting in the repository today, and it is folded into item/release-v0-1-0 rather than filed separately, because 'prove the release pipeline' is exactly what an ungated pipeline fails to do.\n\nThe move costs little: compose-secret, version-from-tag and changelog-release-notes are all S, unblocked, and touch a disjoint file set from the API chain, so with two sessions writing to main they can run alongside it rather than displacing it.\n\nNew order 48-52: compose-secret, version-from-tag, changelog-release-notes, release-v0-1-0, spec-reconcile. The API and docs chain follows at 53-58. Completed items 62 and 63 shifted to 63 and 64 to open the slot; that renumbering is plan position, not history, and no recorded run's touched list is affected because those name ids, not orders.\n\nThree doneWhens must be restated before delegation, per decision/0068, because they are unsatisfiable or unstated as written: item/compose-secret cites a smoke job that boots compose, and no such job exists; item/version-from-tag has to pick 0.1.0 or 0.1.0-dev and needs the image to carry an org.opencontainers.image.version label before the version pill check is anything but decorative; item/image-size has no budget number, and inventing one now would be a guess dressed as a requirement -- the budget should be the size measured at item start, which no later change may exceed without a decision.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:15-milestones"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/release-v0-1-0",
        "item/compose-secret",
        "item/version-from-tag",
        "item/changelog-release-notes",
        "item/image-size",
        "item/api-sanctum-tokens"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0076",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T17:04:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "arm64 is booted natively in CI or the claim is dropped -- never emulated",
      "rationale": "item/release-v0-1-0 and item/tag-v1-0-0 both say the image 'boots to the installer on amd64 and arm64'. release.yml publishes linux/amd64,linux/arm64 through QEMU, and tests.yml's image job builds and boots amd64 ONLY -- its own comment says so. No arm64 FrankenPHP, MinIO, tesseract or SQLite FTS5 binary has ever been executed in this project.\n\nBy this ledger's own standard that is not a weak assertion, it is no assertion. decision/0012 says an unmutated assertion is not evidence; an assertion that was never run is further down than that, and a dual-arch manifest published on it is a claim to users that nothing backs.\n\nThe repository is public -- verified, not assumed -- so GitHub's ubuntu-24.04-arm runners are available at no cost. The honest treatment is therefore to EXERCISE the claim: an image-arm64 job doing a native linux/arm64 build, then the existing first-screen shell smoke (installer served, container still running) factored into a script both image jobs call. Deliberately not the full Playwright drive: the arch-dependent risk is binaries loading and the MinIO COPY --from resolving per platform, not Livewire logic, and duplicating the whole smoke doubles the slowest job for no added signal.\n\nBooting under QEMU is REJECTED rather than merely dispreferred. FrankenPHP and MinIO are Go binaries under qemu-user plus supervisor; a failure there is ambiguous between an emulation bug and an image bug, so it cannot be read in either direction -- the defect decision/0018 names, where a refusal two mechanisms both produce proves neither.\n\nIf the native runner turns out to be unavailable, the fallback is not to keep the claim quietly: drop linux/arm64 from release.yml, strike arm64 from both items' doneWhen, say amd64-only in the README, and file arm64 as post-v1. An honest single-arch image beats a dual-arch manifest nobody has booted.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/release-v0-1-0",
        "item/tag-v1-0-0",
        "item/container-smoke-ci"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0077",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T17:05:00Z",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "name": "The spec is reconciled in its own change, never inside the audit that reads it",
      "rationale": "The spec has drifted from the product in three verified places: base image 8.4 against a Dockerfile on 8.5; the section 2 decision table still specifying Laravel Scout, which is not installed, while line 426 of the SAME document already says extraction goes through the project's own seam 'not through Laravel Scout as originally specified'; and FTS5 listed as a post-v1 optimisation though Fts5SearchIndex has shipped and line 438 describes it. The document contradicts itself, and section 2 is the half a reader meets first.\n\nThis matters twice over, which is why it is an item and not a chore. The spec is the standard item/v1-audit measures the product against, AND it is the text LOOP.md step 5 briefs workers with -- so a worker briefed on section 13 today is briefed on things that are not true. The reorder in decision/0075 sharpens this: the release items now run FIRST and section 13 is their section.\n\nTHE RULE: reconciliation is a separate change from the audit. Folding it in makes the auditor the editor of the standard it audits against -- the conflict decision/0068 names -- and afterwards 'the requirement was edited to fit what was built' is indistinguishable from 'the original clause was met'. Its own item, its own PR, so the edit is visible as an edit.\n\nTwo constraints the item carries. Spec headings are load-bearing: LedgerValidator's spec-anchor rule derives valid isBasedOn anchors from them, so renaming or removing one reddens ledger.yml -- edit content only. And section 2 is a decision table cited by decisions 0001 through 0004, so a superseded row is marked superseded with a pointer to the section that now governs, never deleted: the spec is a design record, and a row that silently changes loses why the original choice was made.\n\nStated plainly rather than papered over: there is no test for prose. The validator passing proves only that no anchor broke, not that the text is now true. The check on the text is that it stays a small, reviewable, doc-only diff.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:12-documentation"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/spec-reconcile",
        "item/v1-audit"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    }
  ],
