    {
      "@id": "mutation/0017",
      "@type": "Mutation",
      "implements": [
        "item/admin-instance-settings"
      ],
      "pullRequest": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/193",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "headSha": "93f1f2611a6fa85974878f65435eaa263a8c12b4",
      "mutant": "Removed only the `wire:submit=\"saveSignup\"` attribute from the public-signup form in resources/views/livewire/admin/instance-settings.blade.php. The form still renders, still carries its data-test, its checkbox still binds and its button still clicks -- it simply no longer reaches the component. App\\Livewire\\Admin\\InstanceSettings::saveSignup() is untouched, so AdminInstanceSettingsTest, which calls that method directly through Livewire's test harness, cannot see it, and neither can guards, lint or any test matrix job.",
      "check": "container-smoke.mjs checkAdminInstanceSettingsPage() -- the image job failed at the doneWhen itself: \"enabling auth.public_signup through /admin/settings never made /register answer 200 for a guest -- got HTTP 404\". WHICH HALF FAILED IS THE POINT. The check asks an anonymous, cookie-less browser context for /register before and after the toggle; the 404-before control PASSED and the 200-after evidence FAILED, which is the right way round. CLAUDE.md records a case where the resting-state half of a smoke pair was argued to be load-bearing and mutation proved the reverse; here the half that requires the feature to DO something is the half that broke. The check also got past checkAdminPeriodsPage, including the purge control's disabled state and its retention blocker, so it reached its own assertion rather than falling over on the way. Every other check on the run passed. Run https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/actions/runs/35338752846.",
      "verdict": "negative",
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "mutation/0018",
      "@type": "Mutation",
      "implements": [
        "item/email-verification-decided"
      ],
      "pullRequest": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/196",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "headSha": "c4ea8ddfc45eca5db3a71f795b5cc44dc4073f41",
      "mutant": "Removed the single line `$user->forceFill(['email_verified_at' => now()])->save();` from App\\Livewire\\Setup\\FirstRun::submit(), so the installer no longer vouches for the administrator it creates. Intended to show checkFirstAdminIsVerified() load-bearing.",
      "check": "VOID, and the reason is the finding. The image job went red but NOT at the named check -- it failed one step earlier, in the smoke's installer helper, with the whole output being: [setup] opening /setup / [setup] submitting the administrator form / page.waitForURL: Timeout 15000ms exceeded. FirstRun::submit() logs the new administrator in and redirects to /files, which THIS ITEM put behind `verified`; unverified, that redirect bounces to the verification notice and waitForURL times out before any later check runs. decision/0035 is explicit that a red job alone proves nothing and the failure must land AT the named assertion, so this run does not show that check load-bearing. Nor can a better mutant: any change leaving the first administrator unverified breaks the installer's own redirect first, by construction, so checkFirstAdminIsVerified CANNOT fail before the installer does. Two predictions recorded in the PR before the run were both wrong -- that it would fail at the named check, and that every other job would stay green (tests/Feature/FirstRunSetupTest also failed, precisely and by name, 1 of 832). What the run DID establish: the write is covered twice, by that feature test and by the installer step, the latter with an opaque message. Filed as issue #197. Run https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/actions/runs/35342595999.",
      "verdict": "void",
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "mutation/0019",
      "@type": "Mutation",
      "implements": [
        "item/email-verification-decided"
      ],
      "pullRequest": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/198",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "headSha": "034d907a276996c9f6ccd5ac131381642a1f8baf",
      "mutant": "Removed `verified: true` from the CreateUser call in App\\Livewire\\Admin\\Users::save(), so the member the smoke creates through /admin/users is no longer vouched for. Chosen after mutation/0018 came back void: this half of the design has a named smoke assertion that can actually land, because the smoke logs in as that member and requires /admin/users to answer 403.",
      "check": "container-smoke.mjs checkAdminUsersPage() -- the image job failed at the named assertion: \"/admin/users answered HTTP 200 for smokemember733429273, who holds no users.manage -- expected 403\". Reached, not collapsed on the way: the check got past the installer and the file, trash, search, roles and periods checks, created the member through the real form, confirmed its home directory and grant, logged in as it, and failed on the status. THE 200 IS NOT A BYPASS, and that was verified by reading the check rather than assumed: it uses memberPage.goto() and reads response.status(), and Playwright's goto() follows redirects, so the status is the FINAL response -- the unverified member was redirected to the verification notice and 200 is that notice rendering. The gate held. Recorded because the message reads exactly like a real authorisation bypass and is not one, which is folded into issue #197. Run https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/actions/runs/35343087954.",
      "verdict": "negative",
      "supersedes": "mutation/0018"
    },
    {
      "@id": "mutation/0020",
      "@type": "Mutation",
      "implements": [
        "item/role-permissions-survive-restart"
      ],
      "pullRequest": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/217",
      "run": "run/0020",
      "headSha": "b5638fb1dc042ca5039acfb5c9941d273e54e527",
      "mutant": "Restored issue #214's destructive behaviour in RolesAndPermissionsSeeder::run() -- createRoleIfMissing() replaced by Role::findOrCreate($name, 'web')->syncPermissions($defaultPermissions), so every container boot puts both roles back to the seeder's constants. Three tokens, the exact defect the item was filed against.",
      "check": "TWO independent guards, and the run separates them cleanly. (1) container-smoke.mjs checkRolePermissionSurvivesContainerReplacement() -- the image job failed AT THE NAMED CHECK AND NOWHERE ELSE: the whole setup phase ran to completion, through the last-administrator refusal, closing 2020-01, the public-signup 404/200 flip, the forwarded-proto reset URL and the byte-identical forgot-password answer, and then '[verify] the member role no longer holds periods.manage after the container was replaced -- raw output: HOLDS:no', 'container-smoke (verify) failed: member role lost periods.manage across a container restart'. (2) RolesAndPermissionsTest 'it leaves an operator-edited role alone when re-seeded' -- 1 failed, 1 skipped, 859 passed, so exactly one case out of 860, its own, with 'Failed asserting that an array contains periods.manage'. lint, build assets and guards stayed green, which is what a three-token seeder change should leave. The prediction was recorded in the PR body BEFORE the run, naming the phase, the message and the HOLDS:no line, with the standard stated as: if it fails anywhere other than that named check, the assertion is measuring something other than what it claims. It did not. The other direction was then observed rather than inferred -- the same smoke line reads '-- OK' on the merged fix. Run https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/actions/runs/35370360355.",
      "verdict": "negative",
      "supersedes": null
    }
